WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.800
You know, usually when we talk about a medical diagnosis, there's this expectation of absolute precision.

00:07.160 --> 00:08.360
Right. We want it to be like engineering.

00:08.580 --> 00:10.300
Yeah, exactly. You treat it like engineering.

00:10.460 --> 00:12.820
You fall off a bike, you break your arm, and they take an x-ray.

00:13.020 --> 00:16.980
And the x-ray shows that jagged white line on the black background.

00:17.360 --> 00:18.260
It's undeniable.

00:18.420 --> 00:21.400
Right. The doctor just points at it and says, you know, there it is.

00:22.260 --> 00:27.400
Broken or not broken. It's clean. It's visible. And honestly, it's comforting.

00:27.400 --> 00:30.560
It really is. It gives us this profound sense of control.

00:30.940 --> 00:36.940
I mean, we desperately want the world to be categorized into neat, static snapshots like that.

00:37.200 --> 00:41.860
Totally. We want to look at a problem, put it in a box, and say, okay, I understand this.

00:41.920 --> 00:43.860
But then you step into the real world.

00:43.960 --> 00:49.580
Oh, man. Yeah. You look at things like neurodevelopment or chronic trauma or even just, I don't know,

00:49.660 --> 00:52.400
trying to figure out why your car is making that weird rattling noise.

00:52.720 --> 00:55.940
And suddenly that perfect x-ray machine is completely useless.

00:55.940 --> 01:00.660
Exactly. You lose that perfect, clean snapshot of reality.

01:01.180 --> 01:07.120
And that loss of a pristine picture, that feeling of wading into diagnostic muddy waters,

01:07.340 --> 01:09.620
that is exactly where we're heading today.

01:09.700 --> 01:11.640
Which is honestly the most exciting place to be.

01:11.760 --> 01:17.020
It is. We are taking a massive, mind-bending, deep dive into a hidden architecture

01:17.020 --> 01:19.920
that dictates literally every part of your life.

01:20.020 --> 01:25.300
And it is a blueprint that most of us, you know, we never even realize is operating in the background.

01:25.300 --> 01:31.700
So imagine if the rules keeping a massive Boeing 777 in the sky were the exact same rules

01:31.700 --> 01:36.220
dictating how a global supply chain completely breaks down during a crisis.

01:36.440 --> 01:37.600
Which sounds crazy at first.

01:37.780 --> 01:42.980
Right. And what if those are the exact same mechanisms governing the invisible rules of contract law?

01:43.080 --> 01:46.320
Or how your brain decides which memories to dream about tonight?

01:46.480 --> 01:49.320
Or even how your immune system fights off a flu virus?

01:49.320 --> 01:50.160
It's all connected.

01:50.520 --> 01:54.560
We are even going to pull the camera all the way back to look at how the earth itself,

01:54.640 --> 01:57.920
the literal ground under your feet, records the passage of deep time.

01:58.120 --> 02:02.120
I mean, it sounds like we're comparing apples to airplanes to tectonic plates.

02:02.480 --> 02:03.240
It really does.

02:03.540 --> 02:07.600
But there is a remarkably elegant thread tying all of these together.

02:07.720 --> 02:11.820
And we are exploring this through an absolutely fascinating stack of sources today.

02:11.820 --> 02:15.940
We have six highly detailed interrelated research papers.

02:16.340 --> 02:19.740
And they were all published recently, September 4th, 2026,

02:19.980 --> 02:22.600
by an independent researcher who goes by the name Fliction.

02:22.860 --> 02:25.400
A very mysterious but brilliant body of work.

02:25.540 --> 02:25.920
Truly.

02:26.480 --> 02:30.780
Our mission today is to unpack Fliction's grand unified theory of what they call

02:30.780 --> 02:32.600
contradiction and continuation.

02:33.320 --> 02:34.680
Okay, let's unpack this.

02:35.080 --> 02:37.920
The core premise we are exploring is that complex systems,

02:37.920 --> 02:41.400
whether that's a human body, a society, or a corporate network,

02:42.180 --> 02:44.340
they do not survive by being perfect.

02:44.620 --> 02:46.120
And that is the crucial pivot right there.

02:46.580 --> 02:48.900
We assume survival requires flawlessness.

02:49.100 --> 02:50.760
But they don't survive by never breaking.

02:51.080 --> 02:55.540
According to this research, systems survive through two very specific paired monisms.

02:55.960 --> 02:57.540
The first is admissibility.

02:57.720 --> 02:58.660
Right, admissibility.

02:58.840 --> 03:02.400
Think of admissibility as a hyper-strict bouncer at the door of reality.

03:02.700 --> 03:07.680
It decides what actions, what cells, or what behaviors are actually allowed to proceed into the future.

03:07.680 --> 03:09.400
And the second mechanism is repair.

03:09.680 --> 03:11.200
Which isn't what it sounds like, right?

03:11.300 --> 03:11.740
Exactly.

03:12.240 --> 03:15.060
Repair isn't just fixing a broken part to make it look brand new.

03:15.360 --> 03:20.740
It's the active, messy mechanism that keeps the system moving forward when things inevitably fall apart.

03:20.980 --> 03:23.160
So it's not about making it pretty again.

03:23.380 --> 03:24.320
No, not at all.

03:24.680 --> 03:30.380
The profound shift here is moving our minds away from objects and toward trajectories.

03:30.800 --> 03:34.700
You know, we are trained by our schooling to look at things as static nouns.

03:34.700 --> 03:37.960
Like a contract is just a piece of paper in a file cabinet.

03:38.320 --> 03:38.460
Right.

03:38.680 --> 03:42.320
Or a memory is a digital file stored on a hard drive in your brain.

03:42.440 --> 03:45.300
A rock layer is just a heavy object sitting in the dirt.

03:46.040 --> 03:49.680
But Fliction is arguing that to understand reality, we have to look at verbs.

03:50.060 --> 03:51.060
We have to look at motion.

03:51.320 --> 03:53.820
So we have to stop asking, what is this object?

03:54.080 --> 03:56.360
Yes, and start asking, how does this system keep going?

03:56.520 --> 04:01.140
When faced with immense stress, noise, or outright catastrophic failure,

04:01.660 --> 04:03.640
how does the system maintain its continuation?

04:03.640 --> 04:09.640
And to really ground this in something visceral, we are starting with a high-stakes human environment,

04:10.160 --> 04:14.500
where waiting for a perfect statistical snapshot isn't just bad science.

04:14.880 --> 04:16.760
It is literally a matter of life and death.

04:17.040 --> 04:18.580
We are looking at aviation safety.

04:18.860 --> 04:26.320
Because if you want to understand how a massive, complex system survives imperfect data and constant minor failures,

04:26.780 --> 04:32.820
you look at the people whose daily job is keeping 300-ton metal tubes suspended in the sky.

04:32.820 --> 04:39.980
Aviation is arguably the ultimate modern test case for learning from things that almost went disastrously wrong,

04:40.140 --> 04:41.900
but didn't quite end in a crash.

04:42.240 --> 04:45.340
So Fliction points us directly to how the United States handles this,

04:45.420 --> 04:49.820
specifically focusing on NASA's Aviation Safety Reporting System, the ASRS,

04:50.820 --> 04:53.640
and the National Transportation Safety Board, the NTSB.

04:54.100 --> 04:58.040
Let's look at the ASRS first, because the mechanics of this are wild.

04:58.220 --> 04:58.820
They really are.

04:58.820 --> 05:04.360
They process roughly 100,000 voluntary de-identified reports every single year.

05:04.740 --> 05:09.020
So mechanics, pilots, dispatchers, air traffic controllers, they all voluntarily write in and say,

05:09.080 --> 05:11.680
hey, I messed up, or hey, I almost flew into another plane.

05:11.800 --> 05:15.340
And the brilliant part is that NASA runs this as a neutral third party.

05:15.520 --> 05:18.880
Right. It's not run by the Federal Aviation Administration, the FAA.

05:19.340 --> 05:19.820
Exactly.

05:19.820 --> 05:26.920
So people aren't terrified that an FAA inspector is going to read their report and instantly revoke their pilot's license.

05:27.260 --> 05:31.520
That structural design is genius because it completely removes the punitive threat.

05:31.920 --> 05:37.040
If you want to gather the data on how a system actually operates on a Tuesday afternoon,

05:37.440 --> 05:41.280
as opposed to how the manual says it should operate, you have to grant immunity.

05:41.280 --> 05:46.440
And alongside that early warning system, you have the NTSB handling the actual accidents.

05:46.980 --> 05:50.520
And they operate completely independently from the regulatory bodies, too, right?

05:50.600 --> 05:50.860
Yes.

05:51.400 --> 05:56.560
Their goal isn't to assign blame, figure out who to sue, or deal with court liability.

05:57.200 --> 06:01.420
Their mandate is factual reconstruction and prospective change.

06:01.420 --> 06:05.700
So they just exist to figure out the probable cause and issue safety recommendations

06:05.700 --> 06:08.660
to change the future trajectory of the industry.

06:09.120 --> 06:13.000
But critics look at this, especially the ASRS with all its voluntary reports,

06:13.240 --> 06:16.000
and they hit it with a massive statistical critique.

06:16.140 --> 06:18.020
It's called the denominator problem.

06:18.380 --> 06:20.400
Okay. And if you aren't a statistician,

06:20.880 --> 06:23.700
this basically means that if you have a ledger of incidents,

06:23.960 --> 06:28.640
say 100,000 reports of pilots misreading a specific runway sign,

06:28.640 --> 06:33.700
that massive number means absolutely nothing unless you know the denominator.

06:33.980 --> 06:36.520
Right. You need to know the total number of flights

06:36.520 --> 06:40.400
or the total number of opportunities for that exact failure to occur.

06:40.540 --> 06:43.760
Because if a million planes landed perfectly at that runway,

06:44.340 --> 06:47.100
the 100,000 mistakes are a relatively small percentage.

06:47.320 --> 06:52.380
Exactly. But if only 110,000 planes landed there, it's an absolute disaster.

06:52.540 --> 06:53.800
Almost everyone is crashing.

06:54.000 --> 06:55.860
Right. So without the denominator,

06:55.860 --> 06:58.820
critics argue that an incident count is just a meaningless,

06:59.400 --> 07:01.200
biased collection of scary stories.

07:01.540 --> 07:05.680
It is a pervasive critique, especially right now when engineers try to apply

07:05.680 --> 07:11.080
aviation safety methods to new chaotic fields like artificial intelligence safety.

07:11.160 --> 07:11.760
Oh, absolutely.

07:12.300 --> 07:15.020
The strict data scientists look at voluntary reports and say,

07:15.520 --> 07:17.940
well, if you don't know the exact baseline exposure rate,

07:18.280 --> 07:20.200
your data is epistemically compromised.

07:20.840 --> 07:23.900
You can't build a mathematical model, so the ledger is useless.

07:23.900 --> 07:26.860
But Fliction pushes back completely on this.

07:27.460 --> 07:32.540
Fliction argues that the history of aviation proves those strict data scientists are wrong.

07:32.700 --> 07:38.580
Because a ledger doesn't need to be this pristine, mathematically perfect statistical sample

07:38.580 --> 07:41.200
to actually guide human action.

07:41.440 --> 07:47.260
Right. Aviation survives because it treats these messy, near-miss anecdotes as structured evidence.

07:47.260 --> 07:51.220
They use it to find latent common mode failures hiding in the system.

07:51.520 --> 07:55.940
And that search for lading conditions is what makes the NTSB investigation so rigorous.

07:56.600 --> 07:59.020
Consider Colgan Air Flight 3407.

07:59.180 --> 08:02.400
Right. That was a tragic crash in upstate New York back in 2009.

08:02.660 --> 08:02.900
Yes.

08:03.440 --> 08:05.560
Now, if you only looked at the sharp end of the stick,

08:05.820 --> 08:08.220
meaning the immediate final moments in the cockpit,

08:08.920 --> 08:11.900
you would say the crew responded inappropriately to the stick shaker.

08:12.100 --> 08:14.540
The plane's automated stall warning went off, right?

08:14.540 --> 08:19.160
Exactly. And the pilot pulled back on the yoke instead of pushing forward to regain airspeed.

08:19.400 --> 08:20.940
Which goes against basic aerodynamics.

08:21.040 --> 08:22.940
You're supposed to push the nose down to gain speed.

08:23.140 --> 08:26.200
Right. So the easiest thing to do is just blame the pilots,

08:26.760 --> 08:29.720
close the case, and say human error caused the crash.

08:29.940 --> 08:32.460
That is the trap most industries fall into.

08:32.660 --> 08:34.080
They stop at the cockpit door.

08:34.920 --> 08:40.080
But the NTSB leans heavily on the framework developed by psychologist James Reason,

08:40.080 --> 08:44.480
what most people know as the Swiss cheese model of human error.

08:45.200 --> 08:46.780
They look for the latent conditions.

08:47.440 --> 08:54.120
So they ask, why did this specific crew react in that completely counterintuitive way on that specific night?

08:54.520 --> 08:54.960
Exactly.

08:55.480 --> 08:57.280
And when the NTSB dug in,

08:57.520 --> 09:00.260
they found exhausted pilots who were commuting across the country,

09:00.720 --> 09:01.900
sleeping in crew lounges.

09:02.300 --> 09:05.200
They found problems with the regional carrier's training programs,

09:05.600 --> 09:06.580
professional standards,

09:06.580 --> 09:10.180
and even the regulatory oversight that allowed those grueling schedules.

09:10.180 --> 09:14.440
Wow. So the investigation didn't just point a finger at the sharp end of the failure.

09:14.680 --> 09:18.820
It looked at the entire organizational trajectory that made that failure almost inevitable.

09:19.040 --> 09:21.920
Yes. The failure wasn't just the pilot pulling the yoke.

09:22.600 --> 09:25.920
The failure was a systemic trajectory that placed a fatigued,

09:26.080 --> 09:29.540
inadequately trained crew in a high-stress icing environment.

09:29.720 --> 09:31.880
And because they identified the systemic trajectory,

09:32.280 --> 09:37.500
the recommendations that followed weren't just a memo telling pilots not to pull back on a stalling plane.

09:37.500 --> 09:42.000
No. The NTSB recommendations completely changed pilot record keeping,

09:42.640 --> 09:45.120
mandated massive overhauls in fatigue management,

09:45.500 --> 09:48.200
and altered stall recovery training nationwide.

09:48.400 --> 09:52.460
They changed the rules of admissibility for the entire commercial aviation industry.

09:52.580 --> 09:57.760
Infliction brings up an even broader historical example of this kind of system-wide repair.

09:58.140 --> 10:01.880
The rise of crew resource management, or CRM.

10:01.880 --> 10:07.720
Oh, yeah. If we go back to the 1970s, you had some truly horrific crashes.

10:08.420 --> 10:11.500
Take United Airlines Flight 173 in Portland.

10:11.660 --> 10:12.820
That's a classic example.

10:12.980 --> 10:17.900
The crew became so hyper-fixated on a potentially broken landing gear indicator light

10:17.900 --> 10:19.940
that they circled the airport for an hour,

10:20.140 --> 10:22.320
completely ignored the fact that they were running out of fuel,

10:22.520 --> 10:23.720
and crashed into a neighborhood.

10:23.800 --> 10:26.080
Or look at the Tenerife disaster in 1977.

10:26.440 --> 10:29.200
Right. The deadliest accident in aviation history.

10:29.200 --> 10:33.300
Two fully loaded 747s colliding on a foggy runway.

10:33.620 --> 10:35.740
And when investigators unpacked these disasters,

10:36.040 --> 10:38.940
they realized the problem wasn't the aerodynamics of the planes,

10:39.440 --> 10:42.380
or the weather, or even technical flying skills.

10:42.480 --> 10:44.220
It was the culture inside the cockpit.

10:44.760 --> 10:45.160
Exactly.

10:45.640 --> 10:48.820
Back then, the captain was essentially treated like a god.

10:49.640 --> 10:50.820
If the captain made a mistake,

10:51.300 --> 10:53.820
the junior officer often knew something was wrong,

10:53.820 --> 10:56.040
but the authority gradient was so steep

10:56.040 --> 10:58.300
that they literally did not feel empowered

10:58.300 --> 10:59.740
to speak up and challenge the captain.

11:00.560 --> 11:02.700
So the repair mechanism the industry needed

11:02.700 --> 11:05.600
wasn't stronger aluminum or better radar.

11:05.960 --> 11:08.460
The repair was a fundamental rewrite of human procedure.

11:08.900 --> 11:10.540
That became crew resource management.

11:11.220 --> 11:14.320
CRM explicitly trained crews on how to communicate.

11:14.560 --> 11:16.700
It instituted standard cowlouts, right?

11:16.700 --> 11:16.940
Yes.

11:17.440 --> 11:20.740
It required the co-pilot to actively challenge the captain

11:20.740 --> 11:22.480
if they crossed certain safety parameters.

11:22.480 --> 11:25.660
It created the concept of the sterile cockpit,

11:26.100 --> 11:29.840
where non-essential conversation is banned below 10,000 feet.

11:30.280 --> 11:32.180
So CRM wasn't a technical fix.

11:32.280 --> 11:35.600
It was a profound shift in what was considered admissible behavior.

11:36.260 --> 11:38.280
It repaired the social fabric of the cockpit

11:38.280 --> 11:40.540
so future flights could continue safely,

11:40.800 --> 11:43.380
even when a captain inevitably made a mistake.

11:43.680 --> 11:46.000
But wait, I want to go back to this ASRS data,

11:46.220 --> 11:47.720
this voluntary reporting stuff,

11:47.840 --> 11:50.860
because if we are building an entire safety philosophy

11:50.860 --> 11:52.480
on voluntary complaints,

11:52.940 --> 11:54.820
isn't that just building a massive database

11:54.820 --> 11:56.380
of the squeakiest wheels?

11:56.380 --> 11:58.020
That is a very common critique.

11:58.500 --> 12:00.380
I mean, it feels like trying to judge

12:00.380 --> 12:02.260
a restaurant's overall quality

12:02.260 --> 12:04.840
by only reading its one-star Yelp reviews.

12:05.240 --> 12:07.540
If people only report when they are scared,

12:07.880 --> 12:09.300
or maybe they are just reporting

12:09.300 --> 12:10.980
because filing a NASA report

12:10.980 --> 12:12.640
gives them a get-out-of-jail-free card

12:12.640 --> 12:13.800
for a minor violation,

12:14.280 --> 12:15.380
how do you actually learn

12:15.380 --> 12:17.280
from a skewed archive of anecdotes?

12:17.280 --> 12:19.860
That tension is exactly what Flixion addresses,

12:20.300 --> 12:22.060
and the answer is incredibly elegant.

12:22.860 --> 12:24.720
The ASRS organization itself

12:24.720 --> 12:26.660
explicitly warns researchers

12:26.660 --> 12:29.220
not to use their database to establish prevalence.

12:29.380 --> 12:30.620
Okay, so they know it's skewed.

12:30.780 --> 12:31.040
Yes.

12:31.400 --> 12:32.160
They state up front,

12:32.860 --> 12:34.200
we are not a perfect rate table.

12:34.760 --> 12:36.240
Do not use this data to calculate

12:36.240 --> 12:38.280
the mathematical probability of a crash.

12:38.960 --> 12:40.800
But Flixion argues that this warning

12:40.800 --> 12:42.280
isn't a defect of the data,

12:42.720 --> 12:44.120
it is an admissibility rule.

12:44.120 --> 12:46.000
Meaning the system is deciding up front

12:46.000 --> 12:47.580
what this data is legally

12:47.580 --> 12:49.580
and scientifically allowed to be used for.

12:50.060 --> 12:50.380
Precisely.

12:50.880 --> 12:52.620
It is not admitted into the system

12:52.620 --> 12:54.100
as a global rate estimate.

12:54.600 --> 12:57.400
It is admitted purely as evidence of vulnerability.

12:57.900 --> 12:58.880
Ah, okay.

12:58.900 --> 13:00.500
Because the goal of aviation safety

13:00.500 --> 13:02.420
isn't actuarial closure.

13:03.060 --> 13:05.160
The goal isn't to perfectly calculate

13:05.160 --> 13:08.960
that a misheard clearance happens 0.04% of the time.

13:09.200 --> 13:11.900
The goal is to issue practical warning signs

13:11.900 --> 13:13.480
before the crash happens.

13:13.480 --> 13:17.900
So NASA takes these messy, emotional, voluntary reports

13:17.900 --> 13:20.180
and rigorously codes them

13:20.180 --> 13:22.240
into highly structured taxonomies.

13:22.360 --> 13:22.580
Yes.

13:22.920 --> 13:24.180
They tag the anomaly types,

13:24.280 --> 13:25.020
the weather conditions,

13:25.140 --> 13:25.820
the human factors,

13:25.960 --> 13:27.300
the specific phase of flight.

13:27.700 --> 13:28.860
They even have algorithms

13:28.860 --> 13:30.680
to combine multiple different perspectives

13:30.680 --> 13:32.020
of the exact same event.

13:32.240 --> 13:33.540
So they take the messy anecdote

13:33.540 --> 13:35.820
and force it into a structural grid.

13:36.100 --> 13:37.800
And then they publish the callback bulletin

13:37.800 --> 13:39.900
to share these very specific lessons

13:39.900 --> 13:40.560
with the industry.

13:40.740 --> 13:40.920
Why?

13:40.920 --> 13:42.560
Because if you get a single report

13:42.560 --> 13:44.180
of a pilot mishearing a clearance,

13:44.300 --> 13:45.440
that might just be an anecdote.

13:45.660 --> 13:45.840
Right.

13:45.840 --> 13:48.040
But if you start seeing a recurrent,

13:48.340 --> 13:49.720
coded family of reports,

13:49.920 --> 13:51.540
all showing confusion

13:51.540 --> 13:54.300
at the exact same taxiway intersection in Chicago

13:54.300 --> 13:57.300
under the exact same nighttime weather conditions,

13:57.720 --> 14:00.500
you don't need a perfect statistical denominator

14:00.500 --> 14:01.900
to know you have a problem.

14:02.040 --> 14:03.080
You don't need to know exactly

14:03.080 --> 14:06.080
how many planes successfully navigated that taxiway

14:06.080 --> 14:08.920
to know that the signage is dangerously confusing

14:08.920 --> 14:11.420
and needs to be repainted immediately.

14:12.220 --> 14:12.460
Yes.

14:12.840 --> 14:14.960
You act before the statistical estimate

14:14.960 --> 14:16.040
is fully stabilized.

14:16.480 --> 14:17.700
Because in aviation,

14:18.380 --> 14:19.660
waiting for a perfect census

14:19.660 --> 14:21.240
means waiting for a fireball.

14:21.420 --> 14:21.860
Wow.

14:22.220 --> 14:23.280
The ledger of mistakes

14:23.280 --> 14:25.060
acts as a continuation device.

14:25.700 --> 14:26.820
It's a pragmatic filter

14:26.820 --> 14:28.180
for deciding which disturbances

14:28.180 --> 14:30.000
deserve to reshape future action.

14:30.260 --> 14:31.520
Now, to be fair to the critics,

14:31.660 --> 14:32.520
fliction does include

14:32.520 --> 14:34.680
some vital disconfirming evidence here.

14:34.680 --> 14:37.560
ASRS relies heavily on soft data.

14:38.120 --> 14:39.100
It's self-reported,

14:39.180 --> 14:40.940
often completely uncorroborated

14:40.940 --> 14:42.260
by flight data recorders.

14:42.580 --> 14:44.280
And there is massive underreporting

14:44.280 --> 14:45.480
of certain types of errors.

14:45.840 --> 14:47.520
Plus, modern safety scholars

14:47.520 --> 14:49.540
like Sidney Decker and Eric Hulnigel

14:49.540 --> 14:50.780
have heavily criticized

14:50.780 --> 14:52.040
relying too much

14:52.040 --> 14:54.260
on James Reason's Swiss cheese model.

14:54.860 --> 14:56.360
They argue that thinking of safety

14:56.360 --> 14:58.900
as just a neat stack of static barriers

14:58.900 --> 15:00.940
withhold in them is way too linear.

15:01.280 --> 15:01.520
Yes.

15:01.660 --> 15:03.600
They point out that real human systems

15:03.600 --> 15:04.880
don't fail linearly.

15:05.120 --> 15:05.760
They drift.

15:06.020 --> 15:06.240
Right.

15:06.440 --> 15:07.980
Humans constantly adapt,

15:08.140 --> 15:09.620
cut corners to save time,

15:09.780 --> 15:11.100
and reorganize in ways

15:11.100 --> 15:12.120
the Swiss cheese model

15:12.120 --> 15:13.180
doesn't capture well.

15:13.500 --> 15:15.660
That critique of the Swiss cheese model

15:15.660 --> 15:16.260
is vital.

15:16.780 --> 15:17.600
Safety isn't just about

15:17.600 --> 15:19.140
plugging holes in a static wall.

15:19.500 --> 15:20.580
It's about managing

15:20.580 --> 15:22.300
that constant dynamic drift.

15:22.500 --> 15:22.720
Yeah.

15:23.020 --> 15:24.140
But Fliction points out

15:24.140 --> 15:25.500
that even with that critique,

15:25.700 --> 15:27.100
the core argument holds up.

15:27.180 --> 15:28.320
Because aviation proves

15:28.320 --> 15:29.140
that a data system

15:29.140 --> 15:30.980
doesn't have to be epistemically perfect

15:30.980 --> 15:32.140
to be a working instrument

15:32.140 --> 15:33.160
of learning and repair.

15:33.160 --> 15:33.640
Exactly.

15:34.440 --> 15:34.620
Okay.

15:34.720 --> 15:35.940
So we've seen how aviation

15:35.940 --> 15:37.340
uses procedural changes

15:37.340 --> 15:38.820
like CRM cowl-outs

15:38.820 --> 15:39.700
and new checklists

15:39.700 --> 15:40.820
as its repair mechanism

15:40.820 --> 15:41.860
to survive shocks.

15:42.120 --> 15:43.980
But how do the physical systems

15:43.980 --> 15:45.520
that move our goods survive?

15:45.780 --> 15:46.900
That's a great transition.

15:47.320 --> 15:47.840
I want to move

15:47.840 --> 15:48.720
from the procedural

15:48.720 --> 15:50.640
shock absorption in the sky

15:50.640 --> 15:52.780
down to the physical shock absorption

15:52.780 --> 15:53.420
in the warehouse.

15:54.300 --> 15:56.020
Let's talk about supply chains

15:56.020 --> 15:56.820
and something called

15:56.820 --> 15:57.920
the bullwhip effect.

15:58.440 --> 16:00.200
This is a fantastic application

16:00.200 --> 16:01.020
of the theory

16:01.020 --> 16:02.420
because we are shifting

16:02.420 --> 16:04.100
from repairing human procedures

16:04.100 --> 16:06.380
to repairing the physical flow

16:06.380 --> 16:07.880
of matter across the globe.

16:07.880 --> 16:09.160
The bullwhip effect.

16:09.860 --> 16:11.260
Fliction cites the canonical

16:11.260 --> 16:13.440
1997 management paper

16:13.440 --> 16:14.700
by Hao L. Li,

16:15.160 --> 16:16.200
V. Padmanahan,

16:16.480 --> 16:17.320
and Sun Jin Wang.

16:17.760 --> 16:18.880
And for everyone listening,

16:18.980 --> 16:20.360
if you want to visualize this,

16:20.580 --> 16:22.180
imagine holding a long bullwhip.

16:22.300 --> 16:23.300
A very helpful visual.

16:23.300 --> 16:24.400
You flick your wrist.

16:24.480 --> 16:25.260
This is a tiny bit

16:25.260 --> 16:26.180
of very small movement.

16:26.720 --> 16:27.860
But as that wave travels

16:27.860 --> 16:28.600
down the letter,

16:28.720 --> 16:29.600
it amplifies

16:29.600 --> 16:31.240
until the very tip of the whip

16:31.240 --> 16:32.120
cracks violently.

16:32.420 --> 16:33.960
That is exactly what happens

16:33.960 --> 16:34.840
in a supply chain.

16:35.140 --> 16:35.760
It's terrifying

16:35.760 --> 16:36.960
how accurate that is.

16:37.040 --> 16:37.180
Right.

16:37.320 --> 16:38.620
And customer demand fluctuates

16:38.620 --> 16:39.920
just a tiny bit

16:39.920 --> 16:41.200
at your local retail store.

16:41.480 --> 16:42.840
But as that order information

16:42.840 --> 16:43.540
moves upstream

16:43.540 --> 16:44.400
from the retailer

16:44.400 --> 16:45.960
back to the regional distributor,

16:46.180 --> 16:47.580
back to the massive manufacturer,

16:48.060 --> 16:50.260
and finally to the raw material supplier,

16:50.400 --> 16:52.560
the order sizes amplify wildly.

16:52.560 --> 16:54.240
A ripple at the cash register

16:54.240 --> 16:55.760
becomes a tsunami at the factory.

16:56.320 --> 16:58.000
And in that 1997 paper,

16:58.440 --> 16:59.380
the authors identified

16:59.380 --> 17:01.240
four classic structural causes

17:01.240 --> 17:02.280
for this distortion.

17:02.580 --> 17:02.760
Right.

17:02.840 --> 17:04.840
The first is demand signal processing.

17:05.480 --> 17:06.260
How does that work?

17:06.580 --> 17:07.620
Imagine a retailer

17:07.620 --> 17:09.580
normally sells 10 bicycles a week.

17:10.660 --> 17:11.860
Suddenly, one week,

17:12.200 --> 17:13.040
they sell 15.

17:13.700 --> 17:15.740
The retailer's forecasting algorithm

17:15.740 --> 17:17.180
sees a 50% spike,

17:17.720 --> 17:18.320
gets nervous,

17:18.740 --> 17:20.660
and adjusts their future forecast up,

17:21.080 --> 17:22.020
ordering 20 bikes

17:22.020 --> 17:22.820
just to be safe.

17:23.000 --> 17:23.280
Okay.

17:23.940 --> 17:24.720
Makes sense locally.

17:25.260 --> 17:26.500
But then the regional distributor

17:26.500 --> 17:27.620
sees an order for 20,

17:28.280 --> 17:29.900
assumes a massive biking trend

17:29.900 --> 17:30.960
is sweeping the nation,

17:31.200 --> 17:33.260
and orders 40 from the manufacturer.

17:33.600 --> 17:34.120
Oh, wow.

17:34.200 --> 17:36.320
So everyone is rationally overreacting

17:36.320 --> 17:37.760
to the signal from the node

17:37.760 --> 17:38.760
directly below them,

17:38.800 --> 17:39.640
rather than looking at

17:39.640 --> 17:41.420
what the actual end consumer is doing.

17:41.500 --> 17:41.960
Exactly.

17:41.960 --> 17:43.420
And the second cause

17:43.420 --> 17:44.520
is order batching.

17:44.840 --> 17:46.500
Which is just trying to save money

17:46.500 --> 17:47.100
on shipping, right?

17:47.120 --> 17:47.260
Yeah.

17:47.340 --> 17:48.520
A distributor doesn't want to pay

17:48.520 --> 17:49.420
for a truck to drive

17:49.420 --> 17:50.600
across the country half empty.

17:50.740 --> 17:50.960
Yeah.

17:51.080 --> 17:52.140
It's economically inefficient.

17:52.420 --> 17:52.600
Yeah.

17:52.920 --> 17:53.800
So they wait.

17:54.440 --> 17:55.200
They sit on the orders

17:55.200 --> 17:56.600
until they have a massive batch

17:56.600 --> 17:58.200
to send upstream all at once.

17:58.200 --> 17:59.780
So to the manufacturer,

18:00.160 --> 18:01.180
demand looks like it's

18:01.180 --> 18:03.120
flatlining at zero for weeks,

18:03.120 --> 18:04.140
and then suddenly

18:04.140 --> 18:05.360
a massive spike

18:05.360 --> 18:06.220
hits out of nowhere.

18:06.440 --> 18:06.740
Right.

18:06.740 --> 18:07.860
And the third cause

18:07.860 --> 18:09.220
is price fluctuations,

18:09.800 --> 18:11.100
specifically trade promotions.

18:12.120 --> 18:13.400
A manufacturer might offer

18:13.400 --> 18:15.100
a temporary end-of-quarter discount

18:15.100 --> 18:15.820
on a product,

18:15.980 --> 18:17.080
so the distributor,

18:17.520 --> 18:19.300
acting entirely rationally

18:19.300 --> 18:20.440
to maximize profit,

18:20.920 --> 18:21.600
forward buys.

18:22.040 --> 18:23.440
They purchase a year's wreath

18:23.440 --> 18:24.700
of inventory all at once

18:24.700 --> 18:26.060
to lock in the cheap price.

18:26.140 --> 18:26.480
Exactly.

18:26.760 --> 18:27.840
And this massive order

18:27.840 --> 18:29.200
has absolutely nothing to do

18:29.200 --> 18:29.960
with what the customer

18:29.960 --> 18:30.940
is actually consuming,

18:31.240 --> 18:32.900
but it completely shatters

18:32.900 --> 18:34.120
the manufacturer's visibility

18:34.120 --> 18:35.500
into true demand.

18:35.500 --> 18:36.660
And the fourth cause

18:36.660 --> 18:37.800
is rationing

18:37.800 --> 18:38.660
and shortage gaming,

18:38.780 --> 18:39.460
which I think we've all

18:39.460 --> 18:40.260
lived through recently.

18:40.420 --> 18:41.040
We definitely have.

18:41.060 --> 18:41.900
If a new smartphone

18:41.900 --> 18:42.980
or video game console

18:42.980 --> 18:44.160
is short on supply

18:44.160 --> 18:45.200
and the manufacturer

18:45.200 --> 18:46.020
tells the retailers,

18:46.240 --> 18:47.220
look, we can only fulfill

18:47.220 --> 18:48.980
50% of whatever you order,

18:49.480 --> 18:50.560
what does the retailer do?

18:50.660 --> 18:51.600
They game the system.

18:51.720 --> 18:51.880
Right.

18:51.940 --> 18:52.760
They order double

18:52.760 --> 18:53.840
what they actually need.

18:54.180 --> 18:54.580
Exactly.

18:54.900 --> 18:56.520
Knowing it will get cut in half,

18:56.540 --> 18:57.200
so they end up

18:57.200 --> 18:58.200
with their true requirement.

18:58.560 --> 18:59.600
But the manufacturer

18:59.600 --> 19:00.580
just sees demand

19:00.580 --> 19:01.640
doubling overnight.

19:01.960 --> 19:02.520
And as soon as

19:02.520 --> 19:03.360
the shortage is over,

19:03.540 --> 19:04.660
the retailers cancel

19:04.660 --> 19:05.480
all those phantom

19:05.500 --> 19:07.060
mortars and the manufacturers

19:07.060 --> 19:08.200
left holding the bag.

19:08.600 --> 19:09.440
And beyond those

19:09.440 --> 19:10.900
four mechanical causes,

19:11.640 --> 19:12.580
Fliction brings in

19:12.580 --> 19:13.840
John Sturman's famous

19:13.840 --> 19:15.400
beer game experiment

19:15.400 --> 19:16.520
from MIT.

19:17.240 --> 19:18.220
It's a simulation

19:18.220 --> 19:19.540
where students manage

19:19.540 --> 19:20.720
a beer supply chain.

19:20.840 --> 19:21.860
And what did that show?

19:22.220 --> 19:22.960
Sturman showed that

19:22.960 --> 19:23.920
even if you remove

19:23.920 --> 19:24.740
price promotions

19:24.740 --> 19:26.060
and shortage gaming entirely,

19:26.540 --> 19:27.260
the bullwhip effect

19:27.260 --> 19:28.740
still happens purely

19:28.740 --> 19:29.580
because of bounded

19:29.580 --> 19:30.640
human rationality.

19:31.420 --> 19:32.740
People systematically

19:32.740 --> 19:34.220
underweight the supply line.

19:34.220 --> 19:35.420
Meaning what exactly?

19:35.860 --> 19:36.980
Meaning people forget

19:36.980 --> 19:37.700
about the delays.

19:38.660 --> 19:39.300
You place an order

19:39.300 --> 19:40.060
for inventory.

19:40.560 --> 19:41.780
It doesn't arrive on Tuesday

19:41.780 --> 19:42.900
because of shipping delays.

19:43.260 --> 19:43.960
You panic,

19:44.200 --> 19:45.260
assuming the order was lost,

19:45.440 --> 19:46.720
and you place a second order

19:46.720 --> 19:47.260
on Wednesday.

19:48.000 --> 19:48.920
You completely fail

19:48.920 --> 19:49.780
to account for the fact

19:49.780 --> 19:50.520
that the first order

19:50.520 --> 19:51.360
is still in transit.

19:51.620 --> 19:51.960
Oh.

19:52.160 --> 19:52.920
It's exactly like

19:52.920 --> 19:53.780
standing at an elevator

19:53.780 --> 19:54.580
pressing the button

19:54.580 --> 19:55.280
and when the doors

19:55.280 --> 19:56.580
don't open in five seconds,

19:56.720 --> 19:58.100
you angrily press the button

19:58.100 --> 19:58.960
four more times.

19:59.380 --> 20:00.000
Pressing it more

20:00.000 --> 20:00.860
doesn't make the elevator

20:00.860 --> 20:01.700
travel faster.

20:02.240 --> 20:03.460
It just sends redundant

20:03.460 --> 20:04.760
panicked signals

20:04.760 --> 20:05.540
to the system.

20:05.660 --> 20:06.180
Precisely.

20:06.620 --> 20:08.560
Now, usually in business schools,

20:08.840 --> 20:09.500
the bullwhip effect

20:09.500 --> 20:10.300
is taught purely

20:10.300 --> 20:11.200
as an information

20:11.200 --> 20:12.220
distortion problem.

20:12.680 --> 20:14.040
People misread signals.

20:14.260 --> 20:15.280
But Fliction argues

20:15.280 --> 20:16.740
this is an incomplete picture.

20:17.060 --> 20:17.620
Fliction says

20:17.620 --> 20:18.440
the bullwhip effect

20:18.440 --> 20:19.440
is actually a crisis

20:19.440 --> 20:21.400
of exhausted repair capacity.

20:21.400 --> 20:22.460
And this reframing

20:22.460 --> 20:23.120
is brilliant.

20:23.860 --> 20:24.340
Fliction says

20:24.340 --> 20:25.500
we completely misunderstand

20:25.500 --> 20:27.120
what buffer inventory

20:27.120 --> 20:28.300
actually is.

20:28.900 --> 20:29.060
Right.

20:29.160 --> 20:30.840
Because in modern business,

20:31.000 --> 20:31.580
we are taught

20:31.580 --> 20:33.360
that having extra inventory

20:33.360 --> 20:34.680
sitting on a warehouse shelf

20:34.680 --> 20:35.640
is a sin.

20:36.100 --> 20:37.480
It's expensive slack.

20:37.800 --> 20:39.320
It's a waste of working capital,

20:39.580 --> 20:40.800
a waste of floor space.

20:41.320 --> 20:42.120
But Fliction says

20:42.120 --> 20:43.380
no, that physical buffer

20:43.380 --> 20:44.400
is a system's

20:44.400 --> 20:45.660
local repair capacity.

20:45.660 --> 20:47.360
It is the literal

20:47.360 --> 20:48.340
physical ability

20:48.340 --> 20:49.760
of that specific warehouse

20:49.760 --> 20:51.760
to absorb a demand shock

20:51.760 --> 20:53.040
without screaming upstream

20:53.040 --> 20:53.700
for help.

20:54.000 --> 20:54.920
Think about it structurally.

20:54.920 --> 20:56.060
If a retailer runs

20:56.060 --> 20:57.460
a perfectly lean operation

20:57.460 --> 20:58.860
with zero safety stock

20:58.860 --> 21:00.140
and a customer walks in

21:00.140 --> 21:01.780
and buys just two extra units,

21:02.180 --> 21:03.360
that retailer is immediately

21:03.360 --> 21:04.300
forced into a state

21:04.300 --> 21:05.840
of inadmissible continuation.

21:06.380 --> 21:07.520
They literally cannot

21:07.520 --> 21:08.660
fulfill the next customer

21:08.660 --> 21:09.840
who walks through the door.

21:10.180 --> 21:11.000
Because they lack

21:11.000 --> 21:11.680
a local buffer,

21:11.920 --> 21:12.820
they have no choice

21:12.820 --> 21:14.080
but to immediately export

21:14.080 --> 21:15.360
that volatility upstream.

21:15.680 --> 21:16.280
They have to place

21:16.280 --> 21:17.220
an emergency order.

21:17.560 --> 21:18.180
But if they had

21:18.180 --> 21:20.140
a small strategic buffer,

21:21.020 --> 21:22.680
a physical repair capacity,

21:23.200 --> 21:23.980
they could absorb

21:23.980 --> 21:25.620
that minor hiccup locally.

21:26.220 --> 21:26.920
The manufacturer

21:26.920 --> 21:27.840
would never even know

21:27.840 --> 21:28.380
it happened.

21:28.580 --> 21:29.560
So the Bullup effect

21:29.560 --> 21:30.300
on this reading

21:30.300 --> 21:31.220
is what happens

21:31.220 --> 21:33.360
when continuation fails locally

21:33.360 --> 21:34.780
because shock absorption

21:34.780 --> 21:35.620
has been thinned out

21:35.620 --> 21:36.460
so aggressively

21:36.460 --> 21:39.220
that ordinary daily variation

21:39.220 --> 21:40.260
has to be amplified

21:40.260 --> 21:41.460
and pushed elsewhere.

21:41.700 --> 21:43.160
This brings us directly

21:43.160 --> 21:44.100
to the holy grail

21:44.100 --> 21:45.380
of modern manufacturing.

21:46.280 --> 21:47.640
The Toyota production system,

21:47.740 --> 21:48.720
or TPS.

21:48.960 --> 21:49.720
The birthplace

21:49.720 --> 21:51.320
of just-in-time manufacturing?

21:51.320 --> 21:51.760
Right.

21:52.280 --> 21:53.260
Taichi Ono designed

21:53.260 --> 21:53.800
this system

21:53.800 --> 21:54.920
in post-war Japan

21:54.920 --> 21:55.800
specifically

21:55.800 --> 21:57.220
to minimize inventory.

21:57.880 --> 21:58.560
And on the surface,

21:59.200 --> 22:00.220
that seems to completely

22:00.220 --> 22:01.620
contradict Flixion's point

22:01.620 --> 22:02.660
about needing buffers.

22:03.020 --> 22:03.980
But Flixion points out

22:03.980 --> 22:05.080
the massive difference

22:05.080 --> 22:06.020
between the true,

22:06.180 --> 22:07.020
authentic Toyota

22:07.020 --> 22:07.880
production system

22:07.880 --> 22:08.920
and the cheap

22:08.920 --> 22:10.380
just-in-time knockoffs

22:10.380 --> 22:11.380
that Western companies

22:11.380 --> 22:12.220
tried to implement

22:12.220 --> 22:13.380
in the 1990s.

22:13.700 --> 22:14.660
And understanding

22:14.660 --> 22:15.400
that difference

22:15.400 --> 22:16.100
is crucial.

22:16.100 --> 22:18.240
The popular caricature

22:18.240 --> 22:19.280
of just-in-time

22:19.280 --> 22:20.680
is that you simply

22:20.680 --> 22:22.320
fire your warehouse staff

22:22.320 --> 22:23.880
and cut inventory

22:23.880 --> 22:24.540
to zero

22:24.540 --> 22:25.820
to save cash.

22:26.320 --> 22:27.360
But Toyota didn't just

22:27.360 --> 22:29.160
slash inventory blindly.

22:29.800 --> 22:31.420
They reduced physical stock

22:31.420 --> 22:32.520
while simultaneously

22:32.520 --> 22:33.800
building incredibly

22:33.800 --> 22:35.120
high process discipline.

22:35.340 --> 22:35.620
Yes.

22:35.920 --> 22:36.860
They used physical

22:36.860 --> 22:37.740
Kanban cards

22:37.740 --> 22:39.060
to perfectly synchronize

22:39.060 --> 22:39.500
production.

22:40.520 --> 22:41.540
Any worker on the line

22:41.540 --> 22:42.920
could pull the and-on cord

22:42.920 --> 22:44.380
and stop the entire factory

22:44.380 --> 22:45.680
if they spotted a defect.

22:45.680 --> 22:46.760
And more importantly,

22:46.960 --> 22:48.080
Toyota built deep,

22:48.440 --> 22:49.140
long-term,

22:49.400 --> 22:50.800
highly integrated trust

22:50.800 --> 22:51.700
with their suppliers.

22:52.040 --> 22:53.180
So their repair capacity

22:53.180 --> 22:54.460
wasn't a giant warehouse

22:54.460 --> 22:55.500
full of spare parts.

22:55.940 --> 22:57.360
It was distributed repair.

22:57.620 --> 22:59.180
It was rapid problem-solving,

22:59.300 --> 23:00.140
immediate visibility,

23:00.540 --> 23:01.540
and a social network

23:01.540 --> 23:02.120
of suppliers

23:02.120 --> 23:03.400
who could pivot on a dime.

23:03.620 --> 23:03.920
Yes.

23:04.580 --> 23:05.960
The system absorbed shocks

23:05.960 --> 23:07.460
through organizational agility

23:07.460 --> 23:08.320
and transparency.

23:08.840 --> 23:09.960
The knockoff versions

23:09.960 --> 23:11.220
implemented by other companies

23:11.220 --> 23:12.440
just cut the inventory

23:12.440 --> 23:13.580
to artificially boost

23:13.580 --> 23:14.520
their quarterly earnings.

23:14.520 --> 23:15.480
They didn't build

23:15.480 --> 23:16.860
the deep supplier relationships,

23:17.180 --> 23:18.040
they didn't implement

23:18.040 --> 23:19.420
the and-on-cord culture

23:19.420 --> 23:20.660
of stopping to fix errors,

23:20.820 --> 23:21.600
and they ended up

23:21.600 --> 23:22.920
with incredibly brittle,

23:23.240 --> 23:24.180
fragile systems.

23:24.480 --> 23:25.360
And the world

23:25.360 --> 23:27.100
saw exactly how fragile

23:27.100 --> 23:28.400
during the 2011

23:28.400 --> 23:29.440
Tuchoku earthquake

23:29.440 --> 23:30.660
and tsunami in Japan.

23:31.080 --> 23:32.220
That was a wake-up call

23:32.220 --> 23:33.060
for everybody.

23:33.280 --> 23:34.040
The devastation

23:34.040 --> 23:34.840
from that earthquake

23:34.840 --> 23:36.680
cascaded across the globe

23:36.680 --> 23:38.040
in ways nobody predicted.

23:38.740 --> 23:40.000
Western automotive companies

23:40.000 --> 23:40.800
thought they were running

23:40.800 --> 23:42.000
incredibly lean,

23:42.420 --> 23:42.820
efficient,

23:43.040 --> 23:44.020
globally diversified

23:44.020 --> 23:44.860
supply chains.

23:45.020 --> 23:45.660
But it turned out

23:45.660 --> 23:46.920
they were entirely dependent

23:46.920 --> 23:47.820
on hidden,

23:48.120 --> 23:49.200
single-source suppliers

23:49.200 --> 23:50.300
buried deep

23:50.300 --> 23:51.720
in their sub-tier networks.

23:52.000 --> 23:52.540
For example,

23:52.760 --> 23:53.880
a company called Rinesis,

23:54.040 --> 23:54.920
a critical producer

23:54.920 --> 23:56.540
of automotive microcontrollers,

23:57.000 --> 23:57.800
was hit hard

23:57.800 --> 23:58.440
by the quake.

23:58.860 --> 24:00.160
Because the global supply chain

24:00.160 --> 24:01.060
had optimized away

24:01.060 --> 24:02.100
all their buffer stock,

24:02.100 --> 24:03.120
and because they had

24:03.120 --> 24:04.160
no visibility into

24:04.160 --> 24:05.100
who was actually making

24:05.100 --> 24:06.020
these microcontrollers

24:06.020 --> 24:07.580
at Tier 3 or Tier 4 levels,

24:08.280 --> 24:09.300
car plants in Europe

24:09.300 --> 24:09.820
and America

24:09.820 --> 24:11.120
suddenly had to halt production.

24:11.300 --> 24:12.540
The local component outage

24:12.540 --> 24:13.840
became a global crisis

24:13.840 --> 24:14.660
because the local

24:14.660 --> 24:15.540
repair capacity,

24:15.740 --> 24:17.080
whether that was buffer stock

24:17.080 --> 24:18.460
or alternative

24:18.460 --> 24:19.700
pre-vetted suppliers,

24:19.840 --> 24:20.760
was non-existent.

24:21.380 --> 24:22.440
So if driving

24:22.440 --> 24:23.360
a lean supply chain

24:23.360 --> 24:24.480
is like driving your car

24:24.480 --> 24:25.540
without a spare tire

24:25.540 --> 24:26.880
just to save a tiny bit

24:26.880 --> 24:27.680
on gas mileage,

24:27.840 --> 24:28.660
is the answer

24:28.660 --> 24:29.780
just to hoard everything.

24:30.420 --> 24:31.320
Should companies

24:31.320 --> 24:33.280
just build massive warehouses

24:33.280 --> 24:35.080
and hold three years' worth

24:35.080 --> 24:36.520
of every single microchip

24:36.520 --> 24:37.000
and screw?

24:37.360 --> 24:38.280
Absolutely not.

24:38.640 --> 24:40.260
And the 2020 to 2022

24:40.260 --> 24:41.820
global semiconductor shortage

24:41.820 --> 24:43.060
is the perfect,

24:43.420 --> 24:44.300
painful example

24:44.300 --> 24:45.420
of why hoarding

24:45.420 --> 24:46.300
destroys systems.

24:46.380 --> 24:46.900
Oh man,

24:47.020 --> 24:47.880
the chip shortage.

24:47.980 --> 24:48.540
I remember you couldn't

24:48.540 --> 24:49.060
buy a car,

24:49.140 --> 24:49.920
you couldn't buy a PlayStation,

24:50.260 --> 24:51.120
it was wild.

24:51.220 --> 24:51.420
Right.

24:51.680 --> 24:52.740
When that shortage hit

24:52.740 --> 24:53.760
and the pandemic

24:53.760 --> 24:54.620
disrupted shipping,

24:55.240 --> 24:56.020
companies panicked.

24:56.600 --> 24:57.380
They engaged

24:57.380 --> 24:57.960
in the ultimate

24:57.960 --> 24:58.740
shortage gaming.

24:59.240 --> 25:00.320
They started panic ordering

25:00.320 --> 25:01.120
and double ordering

25:01.120 --> 25:01.820
components from

25:01.820 --> 25:02.920
foundries in Taiwan.

25:03.060 --> 25:03.580
But the panic

25:03.580 --> 25:04.080
didn't create

25:04.080 --> 25:05.360
genuine repair capacity.

25:05.760 --> 25:06.820
It weaponized inventory.

25:07.380 --> 25:08.240
A giant pile

25:08.240 --> 25:09.360
of the wrong microchip

25:09.360 --> 25:10.020
doesn't help you

25:10.020 --> 25:10.800
finish building a car

25:10.800 --> 25:11.360
if you're missing

25:11.360 --> 25:12.880
a different 50 cent sensor.

25:13.180 --> 25:14.220
Hoarding just tied up

25:14.220 --> 25:15.360
billions of dollars

25:15.360 --> 25:16.260
in working capital,

25:16.740 --> 25:17.720
completely distorted

25:17.720 --> 25:18.760
the demand signal

25:18.760 --> 25:19.580
for the foundries,

25:19.900 --> 25:21.660
and made the bullwhip effect

25:21.660 --> 25:22.820
infinitely worse.

25:23.520 --> 25:24.880
Fliction is very clear here.

25:25.600 --> 25:26.460
More inventory

25:26.460 --> 25:28.120
is not simply better.

25:29.120 --> 25:30.120
A tumor is growth,

25:30.280 --> 25:31.220
but it isn't healthy.

25:31.940 --> 25:32.800
Repair capacity

25:32.800 --> 25:33.760
has to be targeted,

25:34.300 --> 25:35.240
it has to be visible,

25:35.540 --> 25:36.460
and it has to be

25:36.460 --> 25:37.520
relationally supported

25:37.520 --> 25:38.260
by the network.

25:38.720 --> 25:39.320
So we've looked

25:39.320 --> 25:40.060
at physical goods

25:40.060 --> 25:40.740
breaking down

25:40.740 --> 25:42.420
and how strategic buffers

25:42.420 --> 25:43.440
repair those breaks.

25:43.960 --> 25:45.100
But physical goods

25:45.100 --> 25:45.640
don't move

25:45.640 --> 25:46.740
without human agreements.

25:47.440 --> 25:48.280
Let's look at

25:48.280 --> 25:49.360
the invisible rules

25:49.360 --> 25:49.880
that govern

25:49.880 --> 25:50.940
how those goods move.

25:51.460 --> 25:52.260
Let's dive into

25:52.260 --> 25:53.140
contract law.

25:53.240 --> 25:53.560
Yes.

25:53.740 --> 25:54.760
Because Fliction argues

25:54.760 --> 25:55.420
that a contract

25:55.420 --> 25:56.480
is not just a static

25:56.480 --> 25:57.140
piece of paper

25:57.140 --> 25:58.040
sitting in a lawyer's

25:58.040 --> 25:58.700
filing cabinet.

25:59.080 --> 26:00.240
It is a meticulously

26:00.240 --> 26:01.260
managed trajectory

26:01.260 --> 26:02.080
of human behavior.

26:02.280 --> 26:03.100
This is one of the most

26:03.100 --> 26:04.080
fascinating applications

26:04.080 --> 26:04.780
of the theory

26:04.780 --> 26:05.620
because it challenges

26:05.620 --> 26:06.240
how lawyers

26:06.240 --> 26:07.320
are fundamentally trained.

26:08.040 --> 26:08.620
In law school,

26:09.000 --> 26:10.220
classical contract doctrine

26:10.220 --> 26:11.140
is taught strictly

26:11.140 --> 26:11.900
chronologically.

26:12.280 --> 26:12.480
Right.

26:12.680 --> 26:13.760
You start with a concept

26:13.760 --> 26:14.640
called formation.

26:15.520 --> 26:16.540
According to the restatement,

26:16.840 --> 26:17.060
second,

26:17.240 --> 26:17.920
of contracts,

26:18.580 --> 26:19.760
to form a contract,

26:20.200 --> 26:21.120
you need an offer,

26:21.360 --> 26:22.760
an acceptance of that offer,

26:23.000 --> 26:23.720
and consideration,

26:24.040 --> 26:24.620
which just means

26:24.620 --> 26:25.920
a bargain for exchange

26:25.920 --> 26:26.460
of value.

26:26.820 --> 26:28.060
And this is heavily taught

26:28.060 --> 26:28.980
as if you were building

26:28.980 --> 26:30.180
a static object.

26:30.640 --> 26:31.900
Does the contract exist?

26:32.000 --> 26:32.580
Yes or no?

26:32.940 --> 26:34.180
Is the object valid?

26:34.640 --> 26:35.640
And only much later

26:35.640 --> 26:36.300
in the semester,

26:36.740 --> 26:38.180
as a completely separate topic,

26:38.620 --> 26:39.440
do you learn about

26:39.440 --> 26:40.120
what happens

26:40.120 --> 26:41.300
when someone breaches

26:41.300 --> 26:42.000
the contract

26:42.000 --> 26:43.340
and what the remedies are.

26:43.740 --> 26:43.940
Right.

26:44.020 --> 26:44.640
It's taught like,

26:44.860 --> 26:45.760
first we build the box

26:45.760 --> 26:46.700
and then six weeks later

26:46.700 --> 26:47.620
we discuss what happens

26:47.620 --> 26:48.940
if someone smashes the box.

26:49.140 --> 26:49.900
But Fliction

26:49.900 --> 26:52.240
completely flips this framework.

26:52.640 --> 26:53.300
Fliction says

26:53.300 --> 26:54.740
formation isn't the creation

26:54.740 --> 26:55.880
of a static object.

26:56.600 --> 26:57.380
Formation is just

26:57.380 --> 26:58.720
the admissibility gate.

26:58.720 --> 27:00.540
It is the strict set of rules

27:00.540 --> 27:01.220
that decides

27:01.220 --> 27:02.300
whether the legal system

27:02.300 --> 27:03.100
will even allow

27:03.100 --> 27:03.820
your relationship

27:03.820 --> 27:05.020
to enter a legally

27:05.020 --> 27:06.000
supervised trajectory

27:06.000 --> 27:06.960
in the first place.

27:07.220 --> 27:08.000
And viewing it

27:08.000 --> 27:09.320
as an admissibility gate

27:09.320 --> 27:10.720
explains why the law

27:10.720 --> 27:11.920
constantly adapts

27:11.920 --> 27:13.360
when reality clashes

27:13.360 --> 27:14.140
with its rules.

27:14.720 --> 27:15.400
Take the classic

27:15.400 --> 27:16.080
common law

27:16.080 --> 27:17.100
mirror image rule.

27:17.340 --> 27:18.060
Oh, I've heard of this.

27:18.280 --> 27:18.760
Originally,

27:19.300 --> 27:19.900
for an acceptance

27:19.900 --> 27:20.540
to be valid,

27:20.760 --> 27:21.660
it had to perfectly

27:21.660 --> 27:22.760
mirror the offer,

27:23.100 --> 27:23.940
word for word.

27:24.460 --> 27:25.640
If I offer to sell you

27:25.640 --> 27:26.580
100 laptops

27:26.580 --> 27:27.320
for $1,000

27:27.320 --> 27:28.580
and you write back,

27:28.720 --> 27:29.560
I accept,

27:30.000 --> 27:30.680
I will buy

27:30.680 --> 27:31.620
the 100 laptops,

27:31.960 --> 27:33.000
but please package them

27:33.000 --> 27:33.840
in blue boxes

27:33.840 --> 27:34.900
instead of brown.

27:35.260 --> 27:36.080
Under the strict

27:36.080 --> 27:36.960
mirror image rule,

27:37.080 --> 27:38.300
you did not accept.

27:38.420 --> 27:38.640
Right.

27:39.000 --> 27:40.020
You legally rejected

27:40.020 --> 27:40.580
my offer

27:40.580 --> 27:41.860
and made a counteroffer.

27:42.180 --> 27:43.180
Which sounds elegant

27:43.180 --> 27:43.820
and clean

27:43.820 --> 27:45.080
in a law school classroom,

27:45.220 --> 27:46.160
but it is an absolute

27:46.160 --> 27:47.040
nightmare for

27:47.040 --> 27:48.500
actual businesses

27:48.500 --> 27:49.500
trying to operate

27:49.500 --> 27:50.300
in the real world.

27:50.300 --> 27:50.800
Exactly.

27:51.200 --> 27:52.260
Because in the real world,

27:52.600 --> 27:53.820
merchants were constantly

27:53.820 --> 27:54.980
sending purchase orders

27:54.980 --> 27:55.720
and invoices

27:55.720 --> 27:56.500
back and forth,

27:56.680 --> 27:57.660
and the boilerplate

27:57.660 --> 27:58.840
fine print on the back

27:58.840 --> 27:59.480
of those forms

27:59.480 --> 28:01.120
never perfectly matched.

28:01.200 --> 28:01.340
Right.

28:01.460 --> 28:02.220
One form said

28:02.220 --> 28:03.020
disputes go to

28:03.020 --> 28:04.060
arbitration in New York.

28:04.140 --> 28:04.740
The other said

28:04.740 --> 28:05.880
litigation in California.

28:06.220 --> 28:06.760
But the merchants

28:06.760 --> 28:08.280
were ignoring the fine print,

28:08.640 --> 28:09.440
shipping the goods,

28:09.500 --> 28:10.520
and paying for them anyway.

28:10.680 --> 28:11.100
They were acting

28:11.100 --> 28:12.360
like a contract existed.

28:12.940 --> 28:13.900
The mirror image rule

28:13.900 --> 28:14.680
was too strict.

28:14.820 --> 28:15.920
It was making actual

28:15.920 --> 28:17.360
functioning commerce

28:17.360 --> 28:18.760
legally inadmissible.

28:18.760 --> 28:20.220
So the law had to adapt

28:20.220 --> 28:20.780
to reality.

28:21.320 --> 28:21.560
Yes.

28:22.560 --> 28:24.000
The Uniform Commercial Code,

28:24.480 --> 28:26.900
specifically UCC Section 2207,

28:27.420 --> 28:28.580
which lawyers affectionately

28:28.580 --> 28:30.120
call the Battle of the Forms,

28:30.460 --> 28:31.680
completely renegotiated

28:31.680 --> 28:32.800
that admissibility gate.

28:32.900 --> 28:34.000
It created rules

28:34.000 --> 28:36.000
that allowed mismatched invoices

28:36.000 --> 28:38.020
to form a binding contract

28:38.020 --> 28:39.120
under certain conditions.

28:39.440 --> 28:41.100
It proves that the legal system

28:41.100 --> 28:42.040
actually wants

28:42.040 --> 28:43.100
commercial continuation.

28:43.800 --> 28:45.860
The law wants the transaction

28:45.860 --> 28:47.900
to survive human slop.

28:47.900 --> 28:49.040
It's like the law saying,

28:49.160 --> 28:50.580
OK, you guys didn't use

28:50.580 --> 28:51.620
the exact same words,

28:51.700 --> 28:52.820
but you clearly wanted

28:52.820 --> 28:53.640
to do a deal.

28:53.760 --> 28:54.620
You're acting like

28:54.620 --> 28:55.220
there's a deal.

28:55.360 --> 28:56.700
So we're going to admit

28:56.700 --> 28:57.740
this into our system

28:57.740 --> 28:58.700
and let it continue.

28:59.400 --> 29:00.180
But what happens

29:00.180 --> 29:01.300
when things go wrong

29:01.300 --> 29:02.160
during the performance

29:02.160 --> 29:03.040
of the contract?

29:03.640 --> 29:04.480
Flishen brings up

29:04.480 --> 29:06.840
a very famous 1921 case,

29:07.260 --> 29:08.420
Jacob and Young's v. Kent.

29:08.620 --> 29:09.440
Ah, the famous

29:09.440 --> 29:10.280
reading pipe case.

29:10.300 --> 29:11.120
I love this case.

29:11.380 --> 29:12.440
So a wealthy owner

29:12.440 --> 29:13.220
hires a builder

29:13.220 --> 29:14.500
to construct a sprawling

29:14.500 --> 29:15.220
country house,

29:15.220 --> 29:16.280
and the contract

29:16.280 --> 29:17.420
explicitly states

29:17.420 --> 29:17.980
in writing

29:17.980 --> 29:19.220
that all the plumbing

29:19.220 --> 29:20.600
must use wrought iron pipe

29:20.600 --> 29:21.920
made by the reading pipe company.

29:22.100 --> 29:22.520
Very specific.

29:22.780 --> 29:23.300
But the builder

29:23.300 --> 29:24.120
makes a mistake.

29:24.560 --> 29:25.560
For some of the plumbing

29:25.560 --> 29:26.620
hidden behind the walls,

29:27.000 --> 29:27.980
the builder accidentally

29:27.980 --> 29:28.880
installs a different

29:28.880 --> 29:29.620
brand of pipe.

29:30.320 --> 29:31.320
It was the exact

29:31.320 --> 29:32.360
same quality,

29:32.540 --> 29:34.220
the exact same market price,

29:34.740 --> 29:36.320
literally indistinguishable,

29:36.500 --> 29:37.400
except for the brand

29:37.400 --> 29:38.380
stamp on the metal.

29:39.000 --> 29:40.140
But the owner of the house

29:40.140 --> 29:41.300
eventually finds out,

29:41.800 --> 29:42.620
gets furious,

29:42.620 --> 29:44.500
and demands that the builder

29:44.500 --> 29:45.940
tear down the finished walls

29:45.940 --> 29:47.500
and replace all the pipe

29:47.500 --> 29:48.600
with the reading brand.

29:48.600 --> 29:50.280
And Judge Benjamin Cardozo,

29:50.480 --> 29:51.320
writing for the New York

29:51.320 --> 29:52.040
Court of Appeals,

29:52.200 --> 29:53.040
says no.

29:53.280 --> 29:54.640
He establishes the doctrine

29:54.640 --> 29:56.020
of substantial performance.

29:56.380 --> 29:57.840
The deviation from the contract,

29:58.080 --> 29:59.100
the wrong brand of pipe,

29:59.180 --> 30:00.640
was not a material breach.

30:00.960 --> 30:02.980
It was a minor venial fault.

30:03.360 --> 30:04.520
The law did not demand

30:04.520 --> 30:05.200
that the house be

30:05.200 --> 30:06.380
literally torn apart

30:06.380 --> 30:08.020
to achieve a bite-for-bite,

30:08.100 --> 30:09.600
perfect, literal restoration

30:09.600 --> 30:10.660
of the contract terms.

30:10.760 --> 30:10.920
Yeah.

30:10.920 --> 30:12.340
Instead, it allowed

30:12.340 --> 30:13.680
the contractual relationship

30:13.680 --> 30:14.760
to continue via

30:14.760 --> 30:16.140
a compensatory adjustment.

30:16.900 --> 30:18.120
The builder just had to pay

30:18.120 --> 30:19.100
the difference in value

30:19.100 --> 30:20.160
between the two pipes.

30:20.420 --> 30:21.320
Which, in this case,

30:21.360 --> 30:22.980
was basically zero dollars

30:22.980 --> 30:23.720
because the pipes

30:23.720 --> 30:25.140
were identical in quality.

30:25.960 --> 30:26.860
Cardozo realized

30:26.860 --> 30:28.240
that tearing down the walls

30:28.240 --> 30:29.620
would be an inadmissible,

30:29.940 --> 30:31.620
catastrophic economic waste.

30:32.160 --> 30:33.300
The legal system

30:33.300 --> 30:34.280
repairs the contract

30:34.280 --> 30:35.220
without destroying

30:35.220 --> 30:36.340
the physical progress.

30:36.800 --> 30:37.620
And this philosophy

30:37.620 --> 30:38.640
applies to how the law

30:38.640 --> 30:39.720
handles remedies, too.

30:39.720 --> 30:41.820
Flexion says legal remedies

30:41.820 --> 30:42.880
aren't just accounting

30:42.880 --> 30:43.660
ledger updates.

30:44.140 --> 30:45.040
They are active

30:45.040 --> 30:46.340
repair operators.

30:46.740 --> 30:48.200
Take expectation damages,

30:48.500 --> 30:49.180
which is highlighted

30:49.180 --> 30:49.920
in the famous

30:49.920 --> 30:51.080
Harry Hand case,

30:51.380 --> 30:52.280
Hawkins v. McGee.

30:52.360 --> 30:54.140
A classic from 1929.

30:54.860 --> 30:55.700
A doctor promised

30:55.700 --> 30:56.400
a young boy

30:56.400 --> 30:57.680
a 100% perfect

30:57.680 --> 30:58.560
or good hand

30:58.560 --> 30:59.820
after a severe burn.

31:00.200 --> 31:01.040
The doctor performed

31:01.040 --> 31:01.820
a skin graft

31:01.820 --> 31:02.660
using skin from

31:02.660 --> 31:03.500
the boy's chest.

31:03.800 --> 31:04.440
But the graft

31:04.440 --> 31:05.280
didn't just fail

31:05.280 --> 31:06.180
to heal perfectly.

31:06.400 --> 31:07.440
The hand ended up

31:07.440 --> 31:08.420
growing thick hair.

31:08.420 --> 31:09.680
A complete disaster

31:09.680 --> 31:10.320
for the patient.

31:10.780 --> 31:11.280
But when the court

31:11.280 --> 31:12.320
calculated the damages

31:12.320 --> 31:13.500
the doctor had to pay,

31:13.840 --> 31:14.640
they didn't just give

31:14.640 --> 31:15.080
the patient

31:15.080 --> 31:16.220
his surgical feedback.

31:16.580 --> 31:17.340
The court awarded

31:17.340 --> 31:18.640
expectation damages.

31:19.240 --> 31:20.120
Expectation damages

31:20.120 --> 31:20.720
are designed

31:20.720 --> 31:21.980
to put the injured party

31:21.980 --> 31:23.300
in the financial position

31:23.300 --> 31:24.300
they would have been in

31:24.300 --> 31:25.180
if the contract

31:25.180 --> 31:26.280
had been performed perfectly.

31:26.820 --> 31:27.920
The law is literally

31:27.920 --> 31:29.060
trying to repair

31:29.060 --> 31:30.180
the licensed future.

31:30.600 --> 31:31.380
It's protecting

31:31.380 --> 31:32.080
the trajectory

31:32.080 --> 31:32.680
that was promised

31:32.680 --> 31:33.200
to the patient.

31:33.340 --> 31:34.240
And what's fascinating

31:34.240 --> 31:35.460
is how that differs

31:35.460 --> 31:36.900
from other repair operators.

31:36.900 --> 31:38.740
The law also has

31:38.740 --> 31:39.920
reliance damages

31:39.920 --> 31:41.600
which just try to put you

31:41.600 --> 31:42.340
back in the position

31:42.340 --> 31:42.880
you were in

31:42.880 --> 31:43.700
before the promise

31:43.700 --> 31:44.380
was ever made

31:44.380 --> 31:45.840
reimbursing

31:45.840 --> 31:47.380
your wasted expenditures.

31:47.860 --> 31:48.880
And restitution damages

31:48.880 --> 31:50.160
which just strip

31:50.160 --> 31:51.200
the unjust enrichment

31:51.200 --> 31:52.500
away from the breaching party.

31:53.400 --> 31:54.020
Each remedy

31:54.020 --> 31:55.420
is a specific tool

31:55.420 --> 31:56.300
designed to preserve

31:56.300 --> 31:57.360
a different invariant

31:57.360 --> 31:58.420
of the human relationship.

31:59.280 --> 31:59.620
But wait,

31:59.800 --> 32:00.480
let me push back

32:00.480 --> 32:01.180
on this whole idea

32:01.180 --> 32:02.040
that contracts

32:02.040 --> 32:03.300
are these living

32:03.300 --> 32:04.640
moral relationships

32:04.640 --> 32:05.340
that the law

32:05.340 --> 32:06.140
desperately wants

32:06.140 --> 32:06.600
to repair

32:06.600 --> 32:07.720
because that flies

32:07.720 --> 32:08.240
in the face

32:08.240 --> 32:09.420
of Oliver Wendell Holmes

32:09.420 --> 32:10.220
and his famous

32:10.220 --> 32:11.140
bad man theory

32:11.140 --> 32:11.680
of the law

32:11.680 --> 32:13.260
or Judge Richard Posner

32:13.260 --> 32:14.140
and the whole law

32:14.140 --> 32:15.060
and economics idea

32:15.060 --> 32:16.060
of efficient breach.

32:16.380 --> 32:17.080
That's true, it does.

32:17.360 --> 32:17.860
Their argument

32:17.860 --> 32:18.660
is that a contract

32:18.660 --> 32:20.100
isn't a moral relationship

32:20.100 --> 32:20.540
at all.

32:20.640 --> 32:21.440
It's just a financial

32:21.440 --> 32:22.240
menu option.

32:22.860 --> 32:23.620
You either perform

32:23.620 --> 32:24.600
the service you promised

32:24.600 --> 32:26.480
or you pay the damages

32:26.480 --> 32:27.200
to walk away.

32:27.340 --> 32:28.240
It's the toll road.

32:28.760 --> 32:29.660
If it is financially

32:29.660 --> 32:30.440
cheaper for me

32:30.440 --> 32:31.300
to breach the contract

32:31.300 --> 32:32.220
and pay you damages

32:32.220 --> 32:32.860
than it is

32:32.860 --> 32:34.180
to actually finish the job,

32:34.180 --> 32:35.480
I should rationally

32:35.480 --> 32:36.320
just breach it.

32:36.420 --> 32:37.480
It's completely optional.

32:38.100 --> 32:38.820
Yes, the efficient

32:38.820 --> 32:39.380
breach theory

32:39.380 --> 32:40.280
is very popular

32:40.280 --> 32:41.460
in economics departments.

32:42.240 --> 32:42.860
But Flixen

32:42.860 --> 32:45.000
explicitly pushes back

32:45.000 --> 32:46.100
on Holmes and Posner

32:46.100 --> 32:47.220
here, arguing

32:47.220 --> 32:48.040
that the actual

32:48.040 --> 32:49.060
structure of the law

32:49.060 --> 32:50.700
does not view breach

32:50.700 --> 32:52.720
as a lawful neutral option.

32:53.020 --> 32:53.580
And the proof

32:53.580 --> 32:54.820
is found in doctrines

32:54.820 --> 32:55.860
like anticipatory

32:55.860 --> 32:56.620
repudiation

32:56.620 --> 32:58.460
established in old cases

32:58.460 --> 32:59.740
like Hoster-Vide-Latour.

32:59.900 --> 33:00.360
Exactly.

33:00.540 --> 33:01.240
How does that work

33:01.240 --> 33:01.700
mechanically?

33:01.740 --> 33:02.720
If we sign a contract

33:02.720 --> 33:03.620
today for you

33:03.620 --> 33:04.340
to paint my house

33:04.340 --> 33:04.880
next month

33:04.880 --> 33:05.580
and tomorrow

33:05.580 --> 33:06.160
you call me

33:06.160 --> 33:06.440
and say,

33:06.540 --> 33:07.460
hey, I changed my mind.

33:07.540 --> 33:08.020
I'm going to breach

33:08.020 --> 33:08.980
the contract next month.

33:09.140 --> 33:09.940
I don't have to wait

33:09.940 --> 33:10.460
until next month

33:10.460 --> 33:10.940
to see you.

33:11.280 --> 33:11.860
The law treats

33:11.860 --> 33:12.940
your verbal announcement

33:12.940 --> 33:15.100
as a present material breach.

33:15.440 --> 33:17.060
It is an inadmissible transition.

33:17.900 --> 33:18.800
The law says

33:18.800 --> 33:19.740
you cannot threaten

33:19.740 --> 33:20.700
the future trajectory

33:20.700 --> 33:21.580
of this relationship.

33:22.060 --> 33:22.860
If a contract

33:22.860 --> 33:24.040
were merely an option

33:24.040 --> 33:24.780
to perform

33:24.780 --> 33:25.960
or pay later,

33:26.320 --> 33:27.420
I would have to wait

33:27.420 --> 33:28.560
until the deadline passed.

33:29.180 --> 33:29.580
And what about

33:29.580 --> 33:30.640
specific performance?

33:30.640 --> 33:32.500
That completely destroys

33:32.500 --> 33:33.700
the toll road theory, too.

33:33.900 --> 33:34.380
Exactly.

33:34.900 --> 33:35.900
In cases where money

33:35.900 --> 33:36.480
isn't enough

33:36.480 --> 33:38.220
to repair the promised future,

33:38.420 --> 33:38.720
say,

33:39.380 --> 33:40.460
a contract to buy

33:40.460 --> 33:41.680
a one-of-a-kind painting

33:41.680 --> 33:42.780
or a specific parcel

33:42.780 --> 33:43.360
of real estate,

33:43.860 --> 33:45.040
the court will order

33:45.040 --> 33:46.120
specific performance.

33:46.720 --> 33:47.340
They will literally

33:47.340 --> 33:48.180
force you

33:48.180 --> 33:49.360
under threat of contempt

33:49.360 --> 33:49.820
of court

33:49.820 --> 33:51.040
to hand over the painting.

33:51.460 --> 33:52.500
If a contract

33:52.500 --> 33:53.420
were just an option

33:53.420 --> 33:54.140
to perform

33:54.140 --> 33:55.020
or pay a fee,

33:55.240 --> 33:56.020
specific performance

33:56.020 --> 33:56.760
wouldn't exist.

33:57.320 --> 33:57.980
The law wants

33:57.980 --> 33:58.520
the trajectory

33:58.520 --> 33:59.800
of the relationship repaired,

33:59.800 --> 34:00.460
not treated

34:00.460 --> 34:01.880
as a financial toll road

34:01.880 --> 34:02.380
where the wealthy

34:02.380 --> 34:03.440
can just buy their way

34:03.440 --> 34:04.240
out of their promises.

34:04.740 --> 34:05.260
So is there

34:05.260 --> 34:06.360
any disconfirming evidence?

34:06.920 --> 34:07.620
When does the law

34:07.620 --> 34:08.780
just throw up its hands,

34:08.920 --> 34:09.300
give up,

34:09.380 --> 34:10.360
and refuse to repair

34:10.360 --> 34:10.880
the contract?

34:11.260 --> 34:12.040
Affliction points

34:12.040 --> 34:12.620
to the doctrine

34:12.620 --> 34:13.240
of rescission.

34:14.060 --> 34:14.980
This happens in cases

34:14.980 --> 34:15.920
of outright fraud

34:15.920 --> 34:16.980
or mutual mistake.

34:17.620 --> 34:19.080
The classic law school case

34:19.080 --> 34:20.180
is Sherwood V. Walker

34:20.180 --> 34:21.300
from 1887.

34:21.460 --> 34:22.560
The barren cow case?

34:22.840 --> 34:23.180
Yes.

34:23.860 --> 34:25.060
A buyer and a seller

34:25.060 --> 34:26.320
agreed to the sale

34:26.320 --> 34:27.540
of a specific cow

34:27.540 --> 34:29.240
named Rose 2nd

34:29.240 --> 34:29.880
of Aberlone.

34:30.540 --> 34:31.200
Both the buyer

34:31.200 --> 34:31.760
and the seller

34:31.760 --> 34:32.960
genuinely believed

34:32.960 --> 34:34.660
the cow was completely barren

34:34.660 --> 34:35.580
and could not breed,

34:35.980 --> 34:36.780
so she was sold

34:36.780 --> 34:38.040
for a very cheap price,

34:38.520 --> 34:39.100
essentially just

34:39.100 --> 34:39.600
for the value

34:39.600 --> 34:40.220
of her beef.

34:40.860 --> 34:41.540
But right before

34:41.540 --> 34:42.440
the exchange happened,

34:42.560 --> 34:43.540
they realized Rose

34:43.540 --> 34:44.780
was actually pregnant,

34:44.920 --> 34:45.500
which made her

34:45.500 --> 34:46.360
a breeding cow

34:46.360 --> 34:47.460
worth roughly

34:47.460 --> 34:48.820
ten times as much money.

34:49.360 --> 34:50.220
The court allowed

34:50.220 --> 34:50.580
the seller

34:50.580 --> 34:51.740
to rescind the contract.

34:52.040 --> 34:52.960
Because the mistake

34:52.960 --> 34:54.300
went to the very root,

34:54.400 --> 34:55.200
the very essence

34:55.200 --> 34:55.920
of the agreement.

34:55.920 --> 34:57.020
They weren't selling

34:57.020 --> 34:57.820
a breeding cow,

34:57.980 --> 34:58.540
they were selling

34:58.540 --> 34:59.340
a beef cow.

34:59.580 --> 35:00.740
The object they thought

35:00.740 --> 35:01.900
they were contracting over

35:01.900 --> 35:03.360
didn't actually exist.

35:03.780 --> 35:04.260
Exactly.

35:04.820 --> 35:05.840
In cases of profound

35:05.840 --> 35:07.160
mutual mistake or fraud,

35:07.480 --> 35:08.320
the law doesn't try

35:08.320 --> 35:09.240
to repair the contract

35:09.240 --> 35:10.260
by adjusting the price

35:10.260 --> 35:10.860
with damages.

35:11.280 --> 35:12.700
It unwinds the contract

35:12.700 --> 35:13.240
entirely.

35:13.700 --> 35:14.300
It pretends

35:14.300 --> 35:15.000
it never happened.

35:15.220 --> 35:15.900
This proves

35:15.900 --> 35:16.880
that some transitions

35:16.880 --> 35:18.540
are so deeply defective

35:18.540 --> 35:19.520
that they were never

35:19.520 --> 35:20.760
admissible to begin with.

35:21.280 --> 35:22.140
You cannot repair

35:22.140 --> 35:22.640
a trajectory

35:22.640 --> 35:23.580
that was fundamentally

35:23.580 --> 35:25.000
flawed at the starting gate.

35:25.000 --> 35:25.600
Wow.

35:26.100 --> 35:26.980
Okay, so we've traveled

35:26.980 --> 35:28.520
from the physical supply chains

35:28.520 --> 35:29.380
that move our goods

35:29.380 --> 35:31.700
to the invisible social laws

35:31.700 --> 35:32.740
that govern those goods.

35:33.380 --> 35:33.880
I want to make this

35:33.880 --> 35:35.000
even more personal now.

35:35.320 --> 35:36.520
Let's look at the actual

35:36.520 --> 35:37.760
biological memories

35:37.760 --> 35:38.460
inside your head.

35:38.520 --> 35:39.740
That's a great section.

35:39.900 --> 35:40.400
Section four,

35:41.000 --> 35:41.900
sleep architecture.

35:42.660 --> 35:43.860
How does the human brain

35:43.860 --> 35:45.060
decide what information

35:45.060 --> 35:46.080
is admissible

35:46.080 --> 35:47.280
to keep for a lifetime?

35:47.960 --> 35:49.580
This is where Flick Sean

35:49.580 --> 35:51.220
targets the dominant metaphor

35:51.220 --> 35:52.440
that almost all of us

35:52.440 --> 35:53.140
use for sleep.

35:53.140 --> 35:54.560
When we talk about

35:54.560 --> 35:55.200
being tired,

35:55.420 --> 35:56.220
we usually rely on

35:56.220 --> 35:57.080
the standard model

35:57.080 --> 35:57.660
for sleep,

35:58.060 --> 35:59.280
which was heavily influenced

35:59.280 --> 36:00.640
by Alexander Borbally's

36:00.640 --> 36:01.580
two-process model

36:01.580 --> 36:02.640
in the 1980s.

36:03.040 --> 36:03.480
Specifically,

36:03.780 --> 36:05.480
we rely on process S,

36:05.640 --> 36:06.340
which is essentially

36:06.340 --> 36:07.460
a hydraulic pressure

36:07.460 --> 36:08.160
valve model.

36:08.200 --> 36:08.420
Right.

36:08.600 --> 36:09.120
Hydraulic,

36:09.160 --> 36:10.220
like a pipe building up

36:10.220 --> 36:10.860
water pressure

36:10.860 --> 36:11.620
until it bursts.

36:11.880 --> 36:12.320
Exactly.

36:12.800 --> 36:13.860
The idea is that

36:13.860 --> 36:14.580
during the day,

36:14.700 --> 36:15.980
as your brain burns energy,

36:16.340 --> 36:17.480
a neurochemical called

36:17.480 --> 36:18.720
adenosine builds up.

36:18.880 --> 36:20.400
This homeostatic sleep need

36:20.400 --> 36:21.380
builds up in your brain

36:21.380 --> 36:22.540
like physical pressure.

36:22.540 --> 36:23.740
And when you finally

36:23.740 --> 36:24.340
go to sleep,

36:24.460 --> 36:26.040
that pressure is discharged.

36:26.540 --> 36:27.540
The valve opens,

36:27.820 --> 36:28.780
the tank drains.

36:29.200 --> 36:30.340
And as we drain the tank,

36:31.000 --> 36:31.960
we cycle through

36:31.960 --> 36:33.940
roughly 90 to 110 minute

36:33.940 --> 36:34.820
cycles of sleep.

36:35.080 --> 36:36.440
We move between NREM,

36:36.740 --> 36:37.800
which is non-rapid eye

36:37.800 --> 36:38.360
movement sleep,

36:38.500 --> 36:39.700
the deep slow wave sleep

36:39.700 --> 36:40.480
that happens early

36:40.480 --> 36:40.960
in the night,

36:41.400 --> 36:42.100
and REM,

36:42.780 --> 36:43.880
rapid eye movement sleep,

36:43.940 --> 36:44.740
the dreaming phase

36:44.740 --> 36:45.420
that happens more

36:45.420 --> 36:46.020
toward the morning.

36:46.320 --> 36:47.120
So under that model,

36:47.280 --> 36:48.420
sleep is just draining

36:48.420 --> 36:49.320
the chemical tank

36:49.320 --> 36:49.980
so we can function

36:49.980 --> 36:50.660
the next day.

36:50.660 --> 36:51.540
That's the popular

36:51.540 --> 36:52.020
conception.

36:52.740 --> 36:53.720
But Fliction argues

36:53.720 --> 36:54.760
this hydraulic model

36:54.760 --> 36:56.440
is constitutively incomplete.

36:57.480 --> 36:58.500
Process S explains

36:58.500 --> 36:59.480
when we feel tired,

36:59.840 --> 37:00.580
and it explains

37:00.580 --> 37:01.640
how deeply we sleep

37:01.640 --> 37:02.460
based on how long

37:02.460 --> 37:03.160
we've been awake.

37:03.340 --> 37:03.980
But it tells us

37:03.980 --> 37:04.780
absolutely nothing

37:04.780 --> 37:05.500
about what happens

37:05.500 --> 37:06.160
to our memories

37:06.160 --> 37:07.280
while we are unconscious.

37:07.620 --> 37:08.340
A pressure valve

37:08.340 --> 37:09.460
is completely blind

37:09.460 --> 37:10.000
to content.

37:10.780 --> 37:11.800
But modern neuroscience

37:11.800 --> 37:12.900
shows us that the brain

37:12.900 --> 37:14.220
is intensely focused

37:14.220 --> 37:15.480
on content during sleep.

37:15.480 --> 37:16.660
And this brings in

37:16.660 --> 37:17.500
a framework called

37:17.500 --> 37:19.300
Active Systems Consolidation

37:19.300 --> 37:20.340
from researchers

37:20.340 --> 37:21.540
like Suzanne Diekelman

37:21.540 --> 37:22.360
and Jan Born.

37:23.140 --> 37:24.140
They argue that sleep

37:24.140 --> 37:25.200
isn't just a biological

37:25.200 --> 37:26.180
pressure release.

37:26.320 --> 37:27.380
It's a highly sophisticated

37:27.380 --> 37:28.540
record and commit

37:28.540 --> 37:29.580
IT architecture.

37:30.100 --> 37:31.540
It is actively managing

37:31.540 --> 37:32.900
and editing your memories.

37:33.420 --> 37:33.560
Right.

37:34.080 --> 37:34.880
And Fliction maps

37:34.880 --> 37:35.580
this perfectly

37:35.580 --> 37:36.380
onto the concepts

37:36.380 --> 37:37.020
of repair

37:37.020 --> 37:38.320
and admissibility.

37:38.760 --> 37:39.720
During the day,

37:39.840 --> 37:40.640
as you experience

37:40.640 --> 37:41.160
the world,

37:41.580 --> 37:42.780
you encode new,

37:43.080 --> 37:44.160
highly fragile memories

37:44.160 --> 37:45.800
in a seahorse-shaped

37:45.800 --> 37:46.620
part of the brain

37:46.620 --> 37:47.720
called the hippocampus.

37:47.880 --> 37:48.880
But the hippocampus

37:48.880 --> 37:49.560
is small.

37:49.800 --> 37:51.280
It's just temporary storage.

37:51.740 --> 37:52.760
It learns fast,

37:52.840 --> 37:53.980
but it forgets fast.

37:54.180 --> 37:56.080
During slow-wave NREM sleep,

37:56.160 --> 37:56.880
that deep sleep,

37:56.940 --> 37:57.540
early in the night,

37:57.720 --> 37:58.560
your brain enters

37:58.560 --> 37:59.940
a massive repair phase.

38:00.640 --> 38:02.300
It selectively reactivates

38:02.300 --> 38:03.260
those fragile traces

38:03.260 --> 38:04.280
in the hippocampus

38:04.280 --> 38:05.360
to see if they should be

38:05.360 --> 38:06.100
moved to the cortex

38:06.100 --> 38:07.320
for long-term storage.

38:07.580 --> 38:08.480
And this reactivation

38:08.480 --> 38:09.380
isn't just a metaphor.

38:09.860 --> 38:10.660
There is a literal,

38:10.880 --> 38:12.620
physical, electrical dialogue

38:12.620 --> 38:13.620
happening in the brain.

38:13.620 --> 38:15.920
It is incredibly precise.

38:16.540 --> 38:17.160
Researchers like

38:17.160 --> 38:18.220
Bernhard Stericina

38:18.220 --> 38:19.240
have mapped

38:19.240 --> 38:20.280
a hierarchical nesting

38:20.280 --> 38:21.220
of brain waves

38:21.220 --> 38:21.800
that looks like

38:21.800 --> 38:23.080
a perfectly timed

38:23.080 --> 38:24.180
biological orchestra.

38:24.800 --> 38:26.940
You have slow oscillations

38:26.940 --> 38:27.880
in the cortex,

38:28.320 --> 38:29.260
providing a massive,

38:29.780 --> 38:31.040
rhythmic up-and-down beat.

38:31.280 --> 38:32.260
Nested perfectly

38:32.260 --> 38:33.740
inside the up-states

38:33.740 --> 38:34.700
of those slow waves

38:34.700 --> 38:36.180
are faster bursts

38:36.180 --> 38:37.240
of electrical activity

38:37.240 --> 38:38.600
called sleep spindles.

38:38.840 --> 38:39.800
And nested perfectly

38:39.800 --> 38:40.820
inside those spindles

38:40.820 --> 38:42.200
are incredibly fast,

38:42.340 --> 38:43.160
sharp-wave ripples

38:43.160 --> 38:44.400
coming from the hippocampus.

38:44.500 --> 38:45.520
So the hippocampus

38:45.520 --> 38:46.940
is firing rapid bursts

38:46.940 --> 38:47.580
of information

38:47.580 --> 38:49.020
directly into the open windows

38:49.020 --> 38:50.140
provided by the cortex.

38:50.620 --> 38:50.900
Yes.

38:51.340 --> 38:51.920
And during those

38:51.920 --> 38:53.220
tiny, sharp-wave ripples,

38:53.700 --> 38:55.440
the actual waking experiences

38:55.440 --> 38:56.320
of your day

38:56.320 --> 38:57.480
are being replayed.

38:57.660 --> 38:57.840
Yeah.

38:58.100 --> 38:58.800
Matthew Wilson

38:58.800 --> 38:59.760
and Bruce McNaughton

38:59.760 --> 39:00.900
did these classic,

39:01.260 --> 39:02.240
mind-blowing studies

39:02.240 --> 39:02.920
with rodents.

39:03.040 --> 39:03.700
They recorded

39:03.700 --> 39:04.920
the exact neuronal

39:04.920 --> 39:05.940
firing patterns

39:05.940 --> 39:06.920
in a rat's brain

39:06.920 --> 39:07.660
while it was learning

39:07.660 --> 39:08.940
to run a physical maze.

39:09.040 --> 39:09.260
Then,

39:09.500 --> 39:10.300
when the rat fell asleep,

39:10.300 --> 39:10.880
they saw those

39:10.880 --> 39:11.820
exact same neurons

39:11.820 --> 39:12.820
firing in the exact

39:12.820 --> 39:13.520
same sequence

39:13.520 --> 39:14.720
just much faster.

39:14.960 --> 39:15.800
The brain was running

39:15.800 --> 39:16.420
the maze again

39:16.420 --> 39:16.960
in its sleep.

39:17.120 --> 39:17.960
And later research

39:17.960 --> 39:19.100
by Gabrielle Girardot

39:19.100 --> 39:19.560
showed that

39:19.560 --> 39:20.660
if you use technology

39:20.660 --> 39:21.960
to selectively suppress

39:21.960 --> 39:23.360
just those specific

39:23.360 --> 39:24.240
sharp-wave ripples,

39:24.640 --> 39:25.840
the rat doesn't remember

39:25.840 --> 39:27.060
the maze the next day.

39:27.520 --> 39:28.320
So Enrem sleep

39:28.320 --> 39:29.620
is literally cross-checking

39:29.620 --> 39:30.380
these new memories

39:30.380 --> 39:31.660
against your existing

39:31.660 --> 39:32.500
cortical network.

39:32.720 --> 39:33.560
It's asking

39:33.560 --> 39:34.720
the admissibility question.

39:35.220 --> 39:36.700
Is this memory important?

39:37.060 --> 39:37.820
Is it admissible?

39:37.820 --> 39:39.300
Can we integrate

39:39.300 --> 39:40.280
this new fact

39:40.280 --> 39:41.780
into long-term storage

39:41.780 --> 39:43.180
without destabilizing

39:43.180 --> 39:43.920
the whole system?

39:44.160 --> 39:44.480
Exactly.

39:44.680 --> 39:45.460
It's an active filter.

39:46.180 --> 39:46.760
And then we have

39:46.760 --> 39:47.460
REM sleep,

39:47.640 --> 39:48.540
the dreaming phase.

39:48.940 --> 39:49.760
Fliction calls REM

39:49.760 --> 39:50.800
the commit phase.

39:51.300 --> 39:52.020
By the time you get

39:52.020 --> 39:53.160
to the heavy REM cycles

39:53.160 --> 39:53.960
later in the morning,

39:54.220 --> 39:55.340
Enrem has already done

39:55.340 --> 39:55.960
the heavy lifting

39:55.960 --> 39:57.360
of filtering and reactivating.

39:57.680 --> 39:59.000
REM takes the memory traces

39:59.000 --> 40:00.040
that actually survive

40:00.040 --> 40:00.960
that Enrem filter

40:00.960 --> 40:02.620
and further integrates them.

40:02.960 --> 40:03.380
This is where

40:03.380 --> 40:04.320
procedural memory,

40:04.520 --> 40:05.720
like learning to ride a bike

40:05.720 --> 40:06.500
or play the piano,

40:06.620 --> 40:07.460
gets smoothed out.

40:07.820 --> 40:08.480
And importantly,

40:08.680 --> 40:10.220
it is where the emotional tone

40:10.220 --> 40:11.860
of a memory is recalibrated.

40:12.320 --> 40:13.400
Matthew Walker's work

40:13.400 --> 40:14.160
on this is famous.

40:14.420 --> 40:15.520
He talks about how we sleep

40:15.520 --> 40:16.500
to remember the facts

40:16.500 --> 40:17.040
of a trauma,

40:17.500 --> 40:18.440
but we use REM sleep

40:18.440 --> 40:19.160
to strip away

40:19.160 --> 40:20.680
the raw visceral emotion

40:20.680 --> 40:21.880
attached to those facts.

40:22.140 --> 40:23.560
Because REM operates

40:23.560 --> 40:24.660
under a totally different

40:24.660 --> 40:25.740
neurochemical environment.

40:26.780 --> 40:27.420
During REM,

40:27.840 --> 40:28.920
your brain has almost

40:28.920 --> 40:30.080
zero noradrenaline

40:30.080 --> 40:30.780
the stress chemical.

40:31.580 --> 40:32.500
It A is safe

40:32.500 --> 40:33.680
neurochemical space.

40:34.100 --> 40:35.620
It biases the continuation

40:35.620 --> 40:36.360
of the memory

40:36.360 --> 40:37.320
in a different way

40:37.320 --> 40:38.320
than NREM does,

40:38.640 --> 40:39.600
allowing you to process

40:39.600 --> 40:40.800
highly emotional events

40:40.800 --> 40:41.640
without the physical

40:41.640 --> 40:42.520
stress response.

40:42.900 --> 40:43.560
Okay, let me make sure

40:43.560 --> 40:44.380
I'm really grasping

40:44.380 --> 40:45.900
how different this is

40:45.900 --> 40:47.000
from the hydraulic model.

40:47.240 --> 40:48.480
If sleep was just

40:48.480 --> 40:49.760
a hydraulic pressure release,

40:50.140 --> 40:51.100
it would be like

40:51.100 --> 40:52.580
emptying your computer's

40:52.580 --> 40:53.720
digital trash bin

40:53.720 --> 40:55.660
to free up hard drive space.

40:55.920 --> 40:57.080
It's a generic flush.

40:57.140 --> 40:58.120
You couldn't selectively

40:58.120 --> 40:59.540
save one specific file

40:59.540 --> 41:00.440
while deleting another.

41:00.780 --> 41:01.640
That is the perfect way

41:01.640 --> 41:02.240
to look at it.

41:02.240 --> 41:03.400
And the ultimate proof

41:03.400 --> 41:04.160
that sleep isn't

41:04.160 --> 41:05.260
just a generic flush

41:05.260 --> 41:06.180
comes from something

41:06.180 --> 41:07.160
called targeted memory

41:07.160 --> 41:07.940
reactivation,

41:08.400 --> 41:09.780
or TMR experiments.

41:09.900 --> 41:11.220
Oh, this is the wild stuff

41:11.220 --> 41:11.820
with the smells

41:11.820 --> 41:12.600
and the sounds.

41:12.960 --> 41:13.280
Yes.

41:13.820 --> 41:15.240
Researchers like Bjorn Rash

41:15.240 --> 41:16.160
and Jonathan Rudoy

41:16.160 --> 41:17.560
did these incredible studies

41:17.560 --> 41:18.960
that basically hacked

41:18.960 --> 41:20.060
the admissibility gate.

41:20.700 --> 41:21.520
In Rash's study,

41:21.640 --> 41:22.740
people sat at a computer

41:22.740 --> 41:23.900
and learned the locations

41:23.900 --> 41:25.400
of card pairs on a screen,

41:25.780 --> 41:26.740
like the game memory.

41:27.220 --> 41:28.400
But while they were learning,

41:28.780 --> 41:29.680
the researchers pumped

41:29.680 --> 41:30.740
a very specific,

41:30.960 --> 41:31.660
distinct odor,

41:32.120 --> 41:33.080
the scent of roses,

41:33.220 --> 41:33.780
into the room.

41:34.440 --> 41:35.640
So their waking brains

41:35.640 --> 41:37.000
subconsciously linked

41:37.000 --> 41:38.300
the smell of roses

41:38.300 --> 41:39.620
to the spatial memory

41:39.620 --> 41:40.360
of the cards.

41:40.480 --> 41:40.880
Exactly.

41:41.300 --> 41:42.080
Then the subjects

41:42.080 --> 41:42.700
went to sleep.

41:43.180 --> 41:44.140
While they were in deep,

41:44.320 --> 41:45.680
slow-wave NREM sleep,

41:46.060 --> 41:46.920
the researchers pumped

41:46.920 --> 41:48.420
that exact same rose odor

41:48.420 --> 41:49.520
back into the room.

41:49.680 --> 41:50.360
And what happened?

41:50.440 --> 41:51.220
When they woke up,

41:51.340 --> 41:52.240
their recall for those

41:52.240 --> 41:53.480
specific card locations

41:53.480 --> 41:55.300
was significantly improved

41:55.300 --> 41:56.380
compared to control groups

41:56.380 --> 41:57.280
who didn't get the smell

41:57.280 --> 41:57.960
during sleep.

41:58.320 --> 41:59.480
And Rudoy did the exact

41:59.480 --> 42:00.120
same thing,

42:00.200 --> 42:01.540
using specific sounds

42:01.540 --> 42:02.780
associated with objects

42:02.780 --> 42:03.360
on a screen.

42:03.580 --> 42:05.300
So by playing a sound

42:05.300 --> 42:06.580
or introducing a smell,

42:06.760 --> 42:07.360
the researchers

42:07.360 --> 42:08.960
were literally hijacking

42:08.960 --> 42:10.640
the brain's admissions committee.

42:11.160 --> 42:12.320
They were artificially

42:12.320 --> 42:13.720
flagging a specific memory

42:13.720 --> 42:14.160
and saying,

42:14.300 --> 42:15.960
hey, prioritize this one

42:15.960 --> 42:16.460
for the repair

42:16.460 --> 42:17.920
and consolidation process.

42:18.340 --> 42:19.320
If sleep was just

42:19.320 --> 42:20.060
a pressure valve

42:20.060 --> 42:21.140
flushing adenosine,

42:21.620 --> 42:22.440
playing a sound

42:22.440 --> 42:23.600
or pumping in a smell

42:23.600 --> 42:25.140
wouldn't do absolutely anything

42:25.140 --> 42:26.500
to save a specific file.

42:26.720 --> 42:27.280
Precisely.

42:27.280 --> 42:29.220
Sleep is a highly selective

42:29.220 --> 42:30.540
active admissions committee.

42:31.140 --> 42:32.340
It is actively deciding

42:32.340 --> 42:33.480
which memories are fit

42:33.480 --> 42:34.080
to continue

42:34.080 --> 42:35.680
and shape your future behavior.

42:36.340 --> 42:38.140
But Fliction does mention

42:38.140 --> 42:39.200
the Tannoni-Sorelli

42:39.200 --> 42:41.100
synaptic homeostasis hypothesis,

42:41.340 --> 42:42.220
which is a big deal

42:42.220 --> 42:42.980
in sleep science.

42:43.360 --> 42:44.540
And that theory is essentially

42:44.540 --> 42:46.180
a global downscaling theory.

42:46.380 --> 42:46.740
It is.

42:46.880 --> 42:47.940
The idea is that

42:47.940 --> 42:48.600
during the day,

42:48.660 --> 42:49.240
as we learn,

42:49.340 --> 42:49.960
our synapses

42:49.960 --> 42:51.020
get chemically stronger

42:51.020 --> 42:51.500
and thicker.

42:51.500 --> 42:52.640
And if they just kept

42:52.640 --> 42:53.640
getting stronger forever,

42:53.840 --> 42:54.760
our brains would literally

42:54.760 --> 42:56.120
run out of physical space

42:56.120 --> 42:56.660
and energy.

42:57.120 --> 42:58.540
So sleep globally scales

42:58.540 --> 42:59.360
all the synapses

42:59.360 --> 43:00.520
back down to a baseline,

43:00.760 --> 43:01.960
preserving the relative strength

43:01.960 --> 43:02.860
of the important ones

43:02.860 --> 43:03.600
while clearing out

43:03.600 --> 43:04.360
the daily noise.

43:04.720 --> 43:05.720
Which is a brilliant model

43:05.720 --> 43:07.000
and it is very hydraulic

43:07.000 --> 43:07.500
in nature.

43:07.940 --> 43:08.900
It's about managing

43:08.900 --> 43:09.960
the total energetic

43:09.960 --> 43:11.020
and physical weight

43:11.020 --> 43:11.940
of the synapses.

43:12.520 --> 43:13.760
And Fliction doesn't dismiss this.

43:13.820 --> 43:14.920
He says we have to stratify

43:14.920 --> 43:15.780
our understanding.

43:15.940 --> 43:16.180
Right.

43:16.540 --> 43:17.200
Process S

43:17.200 --> 43:18.380
and synaptic downscaling

43:18.380 --> 43:19.920
are incredibly accurate

43:19.920 --> 43:21.440
for tracking sleep deprivation

43:21.440 --> 43:23.500
and the raw biological energy

43:23.500 --> 43:24.560
required by the brain.

43:24.760 --> 43:26.260
But they do not explain

43:26.260 --> 43:27.680
the content-sensitive routing

43:27.680 --> 43:28.840
of specific memories.

43:29.540 --> 43:30.180
Sleep is both.

43:30.740 --> 43:32.520
It is a vital hydraulic discharge

43:32.520 --> 43:33.440
of energetic need

43:33.440 --> 43:35.380
and it is a highly specific

43:35.380 --> 43:37.320
content-sensitive continuation

43:37.320 --> 43:38.620
pass for your memories.

43:38.840 --> 43:39.520
So tonight,

43:39.640 --> 43:40.560
when you hit that deep

43:40.560 --> 43:41.480
slow-wave sleep,

43:41.640 --> 43:42.840
your brain is actively

43:42.840 --> 43:44.380
doing this triage.

43:44.620 --> 43:45.540
It is amazing.

43:46.080 --> 43:47.300
And that transitions us

43:47.300 --> 43:48.760
perfectly from the brain

43:48.760 --> 43:49.420
using sleep

43:49.420 --> 43:50.200
to filter memories

43:50.200 --> 43:50.920
to the body

43:50.920 --> 43:51.920
using the immune system

43:51.920 --> 43:53.260
to filter out physical threats.

43:53.260 --> 43:54.500
Let's look at section five.

43:55.440 --> 43:55.920
Immunology.

43:56.180 --> 43:57.300
This might be the most brutal,

43:57.500 --> 43:57.840
ultimate,

43:58.040 --> 43:59.520
biological admissibility gate

43:59.520 --> 43:59.960
of them all.

44:00.020 --> 44:00.680
It really is.

44:01.120 --> 44:01.980
If sleep decides

44:01.980 --> 44:02.580
which memories

44:02.580 --> 44:03.200
are admissible

44:03.200 --> 44:04.380
to keep your mind coherent,

44:04.880 --> 44:05.980
the immune system decides

44:05.980 --> 44:06.860
which cells are admissible

44:06.860 --> 44:08.480
to keep your physical body alive.

44:08.720 --> 44:09.720
Every time you get a cold,

44:09.840 --> 44:10.640
your body is making

44:10.640 --> 44:11.800
a ruthless calculation.

44:11.800 --> 44:13.840
So Fliction anchors this

44:13.840 --> 44:15.020
in the classic theories

44:15.020 --> 44:16.480
that won the Nobel Prize,

44:16.780 --> 44:18.220
Niels Jern in 1955,

44:18.660 --> 44:20.920
and McFarlane Burnett in 1957,

44:21.360 --> 44:22.780
the clonal selection theory.

44:23.140 --> 44:24.980
This was a massive paradigm shift

44:24.980 --> 44:25.600
in biology.

44:26.280 --> 44:26.900
Before this,

44:27.240 --> 44:28.520
scientists actually believed

44:28.520 --> 44:29.960
in an instruction theory.

44:30.600 --> 44:31.380
They thought the body

44:31.380 --> 44:32.160
saw a virus,

44:32.600 --> 44:33.380
studied its shape,

44:33.540 --> 44:34.880
and then custom-manufactured

44:34.880 --> 44:35.460
an antibody

44:35.460 --> 44:36.580
to perfectly fit it,

44:36.800 --> 44:37.660
like a blacksmith

44:37.660 --> 44:38.960
forging a custom key

44:38.960 --> 44:39.620
for a lock.

44:40.040 --> 44:40.880
Jern and Burnett

44:40.880 --> 44:42.100
completely flipped that.

44:42.320 --> 44:43.500
They proved that your body

44:43.500 --> 44:44.380
already contains

44:44.380 --> 44:45.200
a massive,

44:45.480 --> 44:46.400
random library

44:46.400 --> 44:48.580
of millions of naive B-cells,

44:48.780 --> 44:50.120
each with a totally different,

44:50.300 --> 44:51.620
randomly-generated receptor.

44:52.020 --> 44:52.900
When a virus enters,

44:53.060 --> 44:54.040
the body doesn't build

44:54.040 --> 44:54.780
a custom key.

44:55.120 --> 44:56.320
The virus just bounces around

44:56.320 --> 44:57.360
until it randomly bumps

44:57.360 --> 44:58.140
into the one B-cell

44:58.140 --> 44:59.160
that happens to have a receptor

44:59.160 --> 45:00.060
that kind of fits it,

45:00.400 --> 45:01.760
and then that specific cell

45:01.760 --> 45:03.260
is selected to clone itself rapidly.

45:03.440 --> 45:05.340
It's a Darwinian evolutionary process

45:05.340 --> 45:06.460
happening in real time

45:06.460 --> 45:07.900
inside your own lymph nodes.

45:08.320 --> 45:08.700
Exactly.

45:09.200 --> 45:10.020
And this leads to

45:10.020 --> 45:11.060
an incredible process

45:11.060 --> 45:12.560
called affinity maturation

45:12.560 --> 45:14.520
inside the germinal centers

45:14.520 --> 45:15.560
of your lymph nodes.

45:16.220 --> 45:16.780
Germinal centers

45:16.780 --> 45:17.700
are these temporary

45:17.700 --> 45:18.420
microstructures

45:18.420 --> 45:19.080
that form

45:19.080 --> 45:19.940
when you get an infection.

45:20.360 --> 45:21.380
And this process

45:21.380 --> 45:22.740
is almost universally taught

45:22.740 --> 45:23.800
in biology textbooks

45:23.800 --> 45:25.280
as a pure optimization

45:25.280 --> 45:26.100
search engine.

45:26.460 --> 45:26.940
Right.

45:27.140 --> 45:28.300
It's Ceres taught

45:28.300 --> 45:29.120
as the body

45:29.120 --> 45:29.720
trying to find

45:29.720 --> 45:31.220
the absolute best,

45:31.480 --> 45:32.540
stickiest antibody

45:32.540 --> 45:34.060
to neutralize the threat.

45:34.300 --> 45:34.580
Yes.

45:35.160 --> 45:36.320
The B-cells enter a region

45:36.320 --> 45:37.500
called the dark zone

45:37.500 --> 45:38.560
of the germinal center

45:38.560 --> 45:39.740
and divide rapidly.

45:40.200 --> 45:40.860
As they divide,

45:40.980 --> 45:43.120
their DNA literally mutates

45:43.120 --> 45:43.720
on purpose

45:43.720 --> 45:44.800
a process called

45:44.800 --> 45:46.100
somatic hypermutation.

45:46.580 --> 45:47.300
This is driven

45:47.300 --> 45:48.720
by an enzyme called AID.

45:49.020 --> 45:49.820
AID is essentially

45:49.820 --> 45:51.740
a reckless biological copy editor

45:51.740 --> 45:53.380
that intentionally introduces

45:53.380 --> 45:55.240
typos into the B-cell's DNA,

45:55.660 --> 45:56.520
mutating the receptor

45:56.520 --> 45:58.000
to change its shape slightly.

45:58.180 --> 45:58.520
Then,

45:58.660 --> 45:59.480
these mutated cells

45:59.480 --> 46:00.580
move to the light zone

46:00.580 --> 46:01.780
to test those new shapes.

46:02.080 --> 46:03.160
They have to grab bits

46:03.160 --> 46:03.620
of the virus

46:03.620 --> 46:04.540
displayed by special

46:04.540 --> 46:05.820
follicular dendritic cells,

46:06.260 --> 46:06.860
process it,

46:07.120 --> 46:07.680
and present it

46:07.680 --> 46:09.180
to T-follicular helper cells,

46:09.400 --> 46:10.120
TFH cells.

46:10.320 --> 46:11.140
If the new mutation

46:11.140 --> 46:11.840
binds better,

46:12.120 --> 46:13.180
it gets a survival signal

46:13.180 --> 46:13.940
from the T-cell.

46:14.140 --> 46:14.780
It survives,

46:15.020 --> 46:16.240
cycles back to the dark zone

46:16.240 --> 46:16.960
to mutate again,

46:17.120 --> 46:18.340
and gets even stickier.

46:18.680 --> 46:18.880
Okay,

46:18.960 --> 46:20.080
so that sounds exactly

46:20.080 --> 46:21.480
like an optimization engine.

46:21.820 --> 46:22.720
It's an algorithm

46:22.720 --> 46:23.820
finding the highest

46:23.820 --> 46:24.660
affinity binder.

46:25.160 --> 46:26.100
But Flicksheen says

46:26.100 --> 46:27.340
that optimization story

46:27.340 --> 46:28.080
is secondary.

46:28.900 --> 46:29.540
Flicksheen argues

46:29.540 --> 46:30.400
that germinal center

46:30.400 --> 46:31.120
is primarily

46:31.120 --> 46:33.080
a strict admissibility filter.

46:33.720 --> 46:34.360
Because finding

46:34.360 --> 46:35.800
the stickiest antibody

46:35.800 --> 46:37.600
is just a ranking process

46:37.600 --> 46:39.100
internal to a much,

46:39.220 --> 46:40.200
much stricter gate.

46:40.800 --> 46:42.320
A mutated B-cell variant

46:42.320 --> 46:43.240
doesn't just have to

46:43.240 --> 46:44.400
bind well to the virus,

46:44.540 --> 46:45.480
it must remain

46:45.480 --> 46:46.560
physically foldable

46:46.560 --> 46:47.780
so it actually functions

46:47.780 --> 46:49.040
as a protein receptor.

46:49.300 --> 46:50.480
It has to successfully

46:50.480 --> 46:51.140
compete against

46:51.140 --> 46:52.400
all the other B-cells

46:52.400 --> 46:54.120
for a very limited supply

46:54.120 --> 46:55.460
of TFA survival signals.

46:55.820 --> 46:56.660
And most importantly,

46:57.040 --> 46:57.340
crucially,

46:57.740 --> 46:58.300
it must be

46:58.300 --> 46:59.440
non-self-reactive.

46:59.440 --> 47:00.700
Because aid

47:00.700 --> 47:01.800
is randomly mutating

47:01.800 --> 47:02.300
the DNA,

47:02.800 --> 47:03.760
it might accidentally

47:03.760 --> 47:04.620
create a mutation

47:04.620 --> 47:05.820
that perfectly binds

47:05.820 --> 47:06.540
to the virus,

47:06.740 --> 47:08.040
but also binds perfectly

47:08.040 --> 47:09.160
to your own heart tissue

47:09.160 --> 47:10.500
or your own nervous system.

47:10.700 --> 47:11.480
If a mutation

47:11.480 --> 47:12.340
makes the antibody

47:12.340 --> 47:13.460
attack your own body,

47:13.680 --> 47:14.420
it must be

47:14.420 --> 47:15.520
immediately disqualified.

47:15.660 --> 47:16.260
And what actually

47:16.260 --> 47:17.720
happens to the disqualified cells?

47:17.800 --> 47:18.420
How does the body

47:18.420 --> 47:19.100
get rid of them?

47:19.420 --> 47:20.800
They undergo apoptosis,

47:21.300 --> 47:22.120
which is programmed

47:22.120 --> 47:22.880
cell death.

47:23.420 --> 47:24.340
Mechanisms involving

47:24.340 --> 47:25.440
specific proteins

47:25.440 --> 47:26.080
like BIM

47:26.080 --> 47:27.240
or interactions with

47:27.240 --> 47:28.040
FAS and

47:28.040 --> 47:29.200
FAS ligand

47:29.200 --> 47:30.440
trigger a cascade

47:30.440 --> 47:31.320
inside the cell.

47:31.460 --> 47:32.480
These proteins

47:32.480 --> 47:33.640
aren't just random letters.

47:33.820 --> 47:35.440
They are chemical executioners.

47:35.740 --> 47:36.620
They tell the cells

47:36.620 --> 47:37.340
mitochondria

47:37.340 --> 47:37.920
to literally

47:37.920 --> 47:38.920
spill its guts,

47:39.340 --> 47:40.200
activate enzymes

47:40.200 --> 47:41.080
called caspices,

47:41.300 --> 47:42.560
and safely dismantle

47:42.560 --> 47:42.900
the cell

47:42.900 --> 47:43.840
from the inside out

47:43.840 --> 47:44.580
so it can be eaten

47:44.580 --> 47:45.480
by macrophages

47:45.480 --> 47:46.680
without causing inflammation.

47:47.340 --> 47:48.440
Why is the system

47:48.440 --> 47:49.600
so incredibly ruthless?

47:50.080 --> 47:50.800
Why expand

47:50.800 --> 47:52.160
all that biological energy

47:52.160 --> 47:53.460
making these cells,

47:53.580 --> 47:54.460
letting them mutate,

47:54.460 --> 47:56.360
just to instantly trigger

47:56.360 --> 47:57.700
chemical executioners

47:57.700 --> 47:58.520
to dissolve them?

47:58.920 --> 47:59.760
The system is ruthless

47:59.760 --> 48:01.380
because biological persistence

48:01.380 --> 48:02.260
isn't free.

48:02.760 --> 48:03.740
A rogue antibody

48:03.740 --> 48:04.620
with high affinity

48:04.620 --> 48:05.320
for a virus,

48:05.460 --> 48:06.700
but which also cross-reacts

48:06.700 --> 48:07.780
and attacks your own tissue,

48:08.320 --> 48:09.980
is an inadmissible continuation.

48:10.460 --> 48:11.780
If that single rogue cell

48:11.780 --> 48:13.220
survives and clones itself,

48:13.400 --> 48:14.500
it will cause a catastrophic

48:14.500 --> 48:15.400
autoimmune disease

48:15.400 --> 48:16.360
and destroy the host.

48:16.620 --> 48:17.680
Apoptosis isn't just

48:17.680 --> 48:19.100
a failed optimization search.

48:19.240 --> 48:20.320
It is a vital local

48:20.320 --> 48:21.300
repair mechanism

48:21.300 --> 48:22.580
to prevent the ultimate

48:22.580 --> 48:24.100
contradiction of self-attack.

48:24.100 --> 48:24.540
Wow.

48:24.940 --> 48:25.920
Cellular deletion

48:25.920 --> 48:27.440
is a form of systemic repair.

48:27.800 --> 48:28.220
Exactly.

48:28.500 --> 48:29.360
And the system actually

48:29.360 --> 48:30.460
tries to repair the cells

48:30.460 --> 48:31.540
before killing them

48:31.540 --> 48:32.300
in some cases.

48:33.040 --> 48:34.180
During central tolerance,

48:34.340 --> 48:35.380
when B cells are first

48:35.380 --> 48:36.500
being born in the bone marrow,

48:36.900 --> 48:37.780
if a new B cell

48:37.780 --> 48:39.160
is detected as self-reactive,

48:39.480 --> 48:40.840
it doesn't always die immediately.

48:40.980 --> 48:41.720
It can undergo

48:41.720 --> 48:42.740
receptor editing.

48:42.980 --> 48:44.100
The cell literally

48:44.100 --> 48:45.500
pauses its development,

48:46.280 --> 48:47.200
reopens its DNA,

48:47.780 --> 48:48.880
rewrites the genetic code

48:48.880 --> 48:49.680
for its light chain,

48:49.860 --> 48:50.880
and tries to create

48:50.880 --> 48:51.880
a brand new receptor

48:51.880 --> 48:52.940
that isn't dangerous.

48:52.940 --> 48:54.680
It is actively trying

48:54.680 --> 48:55.880
to find an admissible

48:55.880 --> 48:57.600
continuation rather

48:57.600 --> 48:58.520
than just dying.

48:59.340 --> 49:00.820
That is incredible mechanics,

49:01.160 --> 49:02.600
and this idea that the immune system

49:02.600 --> 49:03.880
is managing a historical

49:03.880 --> 49:05.300
ledger of continuations

49:05.300 --> 49:06.840
leads to a phenomenon

49:06.840 --> 49:09.120
called original antigenic sin,

49:09.420 --> 49:10.840
which sounds incredibly dramatic,

49:11.120 --> 49:12.060
but is also called

49:12.060 --> 49:13.260
immune imprinting.

49:13.440 --> 49:14.960
It's a very observable phenomenon,

49:15.160 --> 49:16.180
classic in decades

49:16.180 --> 49:17.420
of influenza research.

49:17.420 --> 49:19.020
When you are exposed

49:19.020 --> 49:20.020
to a brand new,

49:20.160 --> 49:21.300
slightly mutated strain

49:21.300 --> 49:21.800
of the flu,

49:22.120 --> 49:22.980
your immune system

49:22.980 --> 49:24.020
doesn't start from scratch.

49:24.480 --> 49:25.800
It preferentially wakes up

49:25.800 --> 49:26.660
memory B cells

49:26.660 --> 49:28.240
from your prior flu infections

49:28.240 --> 49:29.580
that kind of recognize

49:29.580 --> 49:30.220
the new strain,

49:30.560 --> 49:31.660
rather than taking the time

49:31.660 --> 49:32.620
to train brand new,

49:32.740 --> 49:34.160
perfect, naive B cells.

49:34.560 --> 49:36.200
Infliction cites recent work

49:36.200 --> 49:37.620
by researchers like Idolo

49:37.620 --> 49:39.420
and others showing this happens

49:39.420 --> 49:41.180
heavily with COVID-19, too.

49:41.660 --> 49:42.800
Our recall responses

49:42.800 --> 49:43.740
to new variants

49:43.740 --> 49:44.860
are heavily biased

49:44.860 --> 49:46.140
by our past exposures

49:46.140 --> 49:47.340
to the original strain.

49:47.620 --> 49:48.600
Right, and this proves

49:48.600 --> 49:49.420
Fliction's point.

49:50.140 --> 49:51.000
If the immune system

49:51.000 --> 49:51.960
was a perfect,

49:52.140 --> 49:53.320
mathematically ideal

49:53.320 --> 49:54.560
optimization engine,

49:54.860 --> 49:56.040
it would always build

49:56.040 --> 49:57.240
the absolute best,

49:57.620 --> 49:58.720
most perfect antibody

49:58.720 --> 49:59.720
for the current threat.

50:00.340 --> 50:01.640
But because immune memory

50:01.640 --> 50:03.180
is a continuation ledger,

50:03.660 --> 50:04.820
the system relies heavily

50:04.820 --> 50:05.820
on past winners.

50:06.040 --> 50:06.820
Those past winners

50:06.820 --> 50:07.460
are numerous,

50:07.680 --> 50:09.040
they activate incredibly fast,

50:09.140 --> 50:09.700
and crucially,

50:09.880 --> 50:10.880
they have already passed

50:10.880 --> 50:11.780
the safety filters.

50:11.780 --> 50:12.920
They are proven

50:12.920 --> 50:14.700
to be non-self-reactive.

50:14.860 --> 50:15.660
So the system says

50:15.660 --> 50:17.020
this old antibody

50:17.020 --> 50:17.860
is close enough,

50:18.060 --> 50:19.060
let's just use this

50:19.060 --> 50:20.020
to survive today.

50:20.420 --> 50:21.180
Sometimes this is

50:21.180 --> 50:21.900
highly protective.

50:22.320 --> 50:23.440
Other times it biases

50:23.440 --> 50:24.720
the response so heavily

50:24.720 --> 50:25.740
that the body struggles

50:25.740 --> 50:27.460
to adapt to a novel mutation.

50:27.800 --> 50:29.040
The past physically shapes

50:29.040 --> 50:30.100
this space of available

50:30.100 --> 50:31.500
continuations in the future.

50:31.740 --> 50:32.700
Now, I know there is

50:32.700 --> 50:33.540
pushback on this.

50:34.080 --> 50:34.700
Fliction mentions

50:34.700 --> 50:36.080
classic hapten systems,

50:36.400 --> 50:37.480
which are simplified

50:37.480 --> 50:38.600
experimental models

50:38.600 --> 50:39.820
using synthetic chemicals,

50:40.040 --> 50:41.100
and those models

50:41.100 --> 50:42.700
do show very straightforward,

50:43.120 --> 50:44.860
simple affinity maximization.

50:44.860 --> 50:46.020
And then there's

50:46.020 --> 50:46.820
this fascinating

50:46.820 --> 50:47.940
live imaging study

50:47.940 --> 50:49.320
by Taz and colleagues.

50:50.120 --> 50:50.800
The Taz study

50:50.800 --> 50:51.840
is a crucial piece

50:51.840 --> 50:53.180
of disconfirming evidence

50:53.180 --> 50:54.260
that we have to acknowledge.

50:54.940 --> 50:55.800
They used advanced

50:55.800 --> 50:56.420
live imaging

50:56.420 --> 50:57.460
to physically track

50:57.460 --> 50:58.100
the dynamics

50:58.100 --> 50:59.260
inside a germinal center

50:59.260 --> 50:59.920
over time.

51:00.440 --> 51:01.020
And they found

51:01.020 --> 51:01.720
that a lot of

51:01.720 --> 51:02.940
clonal extinction cells

51:02.940 --> 51:03.660
dying off

51:03.660 --> 51:04.820
didn't actually look

51:04.820 --> 51:05.460
like a perfectly

51:05.460 --> 51:07.080
principled admissibility filter

51:07.080 --> 51:08.180
catching bad cells.

51:08.420 --> 51:09.140
It looked like

51:09.140 --> 51:10.060
stochastic drift.

51:10.380 --> 51:11.100
Pure chance.

51:11.100 --> 51:12.640
Sometimes a perfectly good

51:12.640 --> 51:13.640
high affinity B cell

51:13.640 --> 51:14.300
just dies

51:14.300 --> 51:14.920
because it happened

51:14.920 --> 51:15.520
to be in the wrong

51:15.520 --> 51:16.240
physical place

51:16.240 --> 51:17.000
at the wrong time

51:17.000 --> 51:18.620
and missed a survival signal.

51:18.940 --> 51:19.920
So it's not a perfect

51:19.920 --> 51:21.000
hyper-rational filter.

51:21.160 --> 51:21.680
It's messy.

51:22.020 --> 51:23.260
It is deeply messy.

51:23.740 --> 51:24.640
But Fliction's point

51:24.640 --> 51:25.280
is that while

51:25.280 --> 51:26.140
stochastic drift

51:26.140 --> 51:27.240
certainly exists,

51:27.500 --> 51:28.260
it happens within

51:28.260 --> 51:29.120
the hard boundaries

51:29.120 --> 51:29.880
of admissibility.

51:30.400 --> 51:31.140
A good cell

51:31.140 --> 51:32.320
might die by chance,

51:32.520 --> 51:33.300
but if a cell

51:33.300 --> 51:34.880
is dangerously self-reactive,

51:35.140 --> 51:36.220
the filter will catch it.

51:36.600 --> 51:37.220
The optimization

51:37.220 --> 51:38.620
and the random drift

51:38.620 --> 51:39.580
are nested inside

51:39.580 --> 51:40.740
a much stricter

51:40.740 --> 51:41.780
life-or-death account

51:41.780 --> 51:42.480
of what is allowed

51:42.480 --> 51:43.180
to survive

51:43.180 --> 51:44.700
without destroying the host.

51:45.060 --> 51:45.660
Okay, we've gone

51:45.660 --> 51:47.160
from microscopic cells

51:47.160 --> 51:48.240
executing themselves

51:48.240 --> 51:49.300
to human brains

51:49.300 --> 51:50.040
filtering memories

51:50.040 --> 51:50.940
to social laws

51:50.940 --> 51:52.400
and global supply chains.

51:52.700 --> 51:53.720
Let's pull the camera

51:53.720 --> 51:54.420
all the way back

51:54.420 --> 51:55.780
for our final section.

51:56.120 --> 51:56.720
Section 6.

51:57.720 --> 51:58.240
Geology.

51:58.980 --> 51:59.560
We are looking

51:59.560 --> 52:00.320
at the Earth itself.

52:00.400 --> 52:01.260
How does the planet record

52:01.260 --> 52:02.480
breaks in its own continuation?

52:02.980 --> 52:03.640
We are talking

52:03.640 --> 52:04.580
about deep time.

52:04.920 --> 52:05.840
And Fliction anchors

52:05.840 --> 52:06.700
this final section

52:06.700 --> 52:07.920
in one of the most famous

52:07.920 --> 52:09.060
paradigm-shifting moments

52:09.060 --> 52:10.280
in the history of science,

52:10.920 --> 52:11.900
James Hutton standing

52:11.900 --> 52:13.360
at Sicker Point in Scotland

52:13.360 --> 52:15.000
in 1788.

52:15.240 --> 52:15.700
Sicker Point,

52:16.040 --> 52:17.360
what exactly did James Hutton

52:17.360 --> 52:18.080
see when he looked

52:18.080 --> 52:18.700
at those rocks?

52:19.020 --> 52:20.080
He saw an angular

52:20.080 --> 52:20.860
unconformity.

52:21.520 --> 52:22.580
To visualize this,

52:22.980 --> 52:23.840
imagine standing

52:23.840 --> 52:24.900
on a rocky beach.

52:25.520 --> 52:27.160
He saw near-vertical layers

52:27.160 --> 52:28.900
of older gray rock,

52:29.080 --> 52:30.920
specifically Silurian gray wax,

52:31.340 --> 52:32.560
which are hard mudstones

52:32.560 --> 52:33.700
formed in the deep ocean.

52:34.540 --> 52:35.620
These gray layers

52:35.620 --> 52:36.140
were standing

52:36.140 --> 52:36.960
almost straight up

52:36.960 --> 52:37.780
and they had been

52:37.780 --> 52:38.860
sliced off perfectly

52:38.860 --> 52:39.740
flat on top.

52:40.120 --> 52:40.880
And sitting directly

52:40.880 --> 52:41.620
on top of them,

52:41.820 --> 52:42.600
resting horizontally,

52:42.940 --> 52:44.480
were layers of younger,

52:44.860 --> 52:45.540
reddish rock,

52:45.620 --> 52:47.540
Devonian old red sandstone,

52:47.760 --> 52:48.520
which is formed

52:48.520 --> 52:49.720
from desert river sands.

52:50.200 --> 52:50.920
So you have vertical,

52:51.260 --> 52:52.620
deep ocean gray stripes

52:52.620 --> 52:55.320
with horizontal desert red stripes

52:55.320 --> 52:56.440
resting right on top of them.

52:56.500 --> 52:56.880
Exactly.

52:57.080 --> 52:57.960
And Hutton realized

52:57.960 --> 52:58.640
the immense,

52:58.840 --> 52:59.840
terrifying implications

52:59.840 --> 53:01.300
of that physical contact point.

53:01.760 --> 53:03.760
Usually, geology teaches stratigraphy

53:03.760 --> 53:05.120
as a science of positive,

53:05.320 --> 53:06.100
continuous layers.

53:06.720 --> 53:07.720
Sand settles at the bottom

53:07.720 --> 53:08.180
of a lake,

53:08.260 --> 53:09.100
it turns to rock,

53:09.220 --> 53:10.440
more sand settles on top,

53:10.760 --> 53:11.640
and you read the layers

53:11.640 --> 53:12.320
chronologically,

53:12.480 --> 53:13.240
like reading the pages

53:13.240 --> 53:13.780
in a book.

53:14.160 --> 53:15.080
Unconformities where layers

53:15.080 --> 53:16.300
are missing are often viewed

53:16.300 --> 53:17.340
as missing data,

53:17.480 --> 53:18.760
a regrettable blank spot

53:18.760 --> 53:19.780
in the historical record.

53:19.920 --> 53:20.720
But Flitchin argues

53:20.720 --> 53:21.400
these gaps,

53:21.500 --> 53:22.320
these unconformities,

53:22.440 --> 53:23.680
are actually legible boundaries.

53:23.860 --> 53:25.200
They aren't blank spots at all.

53:25.440 --> 53:25.740
Right.

53:26.400 --> 53:27.200
Hutton realized that

53:27.200 --> 53:27.940
for Sychar Point

53:27.940 --> 53:29.100
to physically exist,

53:29.300 --> 53:30.200
an enormous amount

53:30.200 --> 53:30.840
of violent,

53:31.040 --> 53:31.880
unrecorded history

53:31.880 --> 53:32.640
had to have happened

53:32.640 --> 53:33.280
in that gap.

53:33.640 --> 53:34.960
The lower gray rocks

53:34.960 --> 53:36.040
were laid down flat

53:36.040 --> 53:36.880
in a deep ocean,

53:37.080 --> 53:37.380
buried,

53:37.600 --> 53:38.460
and turned to stone.

53:39.060 --> 53:39.500
Then,

53:39.740 --> 53:41.340
massive tectonic forces

53:41.340 --> 53:42.640
of continents colliding

53:42.640 --> 53:44.140
folded and tilted them

53:44.140 --> 53:45.200
completely vertical.

53:46.060 --> 53:46.560
Then,

53:46.700 --> 53:47.800
they were pushed up

53:47.800 --> 53:48.520
above sea level

53:48.520 --> 53:49.640
to form a mountain range.

53:50.200 --> 53:50.500
Then,

53:50.660 --> 53:51.400
millions of years

53:51.400 --> 53:52.440
of rain and wind

53:52.440 --> 53:53.260
eroded the tops

53:53.260 --> 53:53.960
of those mountains

53:53.960 --> 53:54.880
completely flat.

53:55.480 --> 53:56.100
And then,

53:56.420 --> 53:57.820
the land sank back down

53:57.820 --> 53:58.680
to allow new,

53:58.900 --> 53:59.720
red desert sand

53:59.720 --> 54:00.440
to be deposited

54:00.440 --> 54:01.780
horizontally on top.

54:02.240 --> 54:02.380
So,

54:02.420 --> 54:03.220
it's like opening a novel,

54:03.380 --> 54:04.820
finding chapters 4 through 10

54:04.820 --> 54:05.460
ripped out.

54:05.780 --> 54:06.860
But realizing that

54:06.860 --> 54:07.600
the jagged edges

54:07.600 --> 54:08.460
of the torn pages

54:08.460 --> 54:09.280
and the scorch marks

54:09.280 --> 54:09.860
on the binding

54:09.860 --> 54:10.860
actually tell you

54:10.860 --> 54:11.240
a better,

54:11.360 --> 54:12.160
more accurate story

54:12.160 --> 54:13.040
about a mass of fire

54:13.040 --> 54:13.620
in the library

54:13.620 --> 54:14.780
than the missing text

54:14.780 --> 54:15.480
itself would have.

54:15.660 --> 54:17.120
That is a phenomenal analogy,

54:17.240 --> 54:17.480
yes.

54:17.480 --> 54:18.620
The physical gap

54:18.620 --> 54:19.420
encodes a period

54:19.420 --> 54:20.340
when the local system

54:20.340 --> 54:21.100
could not admit

54:21.100 --> 54:22.320
straightforward continuation.

54:23.080 --> 54:23.560
The environment

54:23.560 --> 54:24.460
violently shifted

54:24.460 --> 54:25.860
from a depositional regime

54:25.860 --> 54:26.560
where rocks are

54:26.560 --> 54:27.220
peacefully made

54:27.220 --> 54:28.160
to an active

54:28.160 --> 54:29.040
tectonic regime

54:29.040 --> 54:29.640
of uplift,

54:29.860 --> 54:30.140
deformation,

54:30.460 --> 54:30.900
and erosion.

54:31.200 --> 54:32.020
The normal rules

54:32.020 --> 54:32.760
of making rock

54:32.760 --> 54:33.780
were totally suspended.

54:34.180 --> 54:34.920
The history became

54:34.920 --> 54:36.200
physically inadmissible

54:36.200 --> 54:36.860
to record.

54:37.120 --> 54:37.600
Exactly.

54:37.960 --> 54:39.000
And the later return

54:39.000 --> 54:39.720
of the horizontal

54:39.720 --> 54:40.500
red sandstone

54:40.500 --> 54:41.900
isn't just more rock,

54:41.980 --> 54:43.000
it is a geologic

54:43.000 --> 54:44.140
repair event.

54:44.700 --> 54:45.560
It's the establishment

54:45.560 --> 54:46.380
of a new,

54:46.580 --> 54:47.920
coherent sequence

54:47.920 --> 54:49.480
across a battered surface

54:49.480 --> 54:50.420
that encodes

54:50.420 --> 54:51.340
the violent history

54:51.340 --> 54:52.040
of what broke

54:52.040 --> 54:52.540
the sequence

54:52.540 --> 54:53.340
in the first place.

54:53.560 --> 54:54.580
Flixian scales

54:54.580 --> 54:55.460
this concept up

54:55.460 --> 54:56.120
to something called

54:56.120 --> 54:57.300
the Great Unconformity

54:57.300 --> 54:58.140
in the Grand Canyon,

54:58.500 --> 54:59.300
which is truly

54:59.300 --> 54:59.920
mind-blowing.

55:00.460 --> 55:00.820
If you go to

55:00.820 --> 55:01.480
the Grand Canyon,

55:01.700 --> 55:02.100
you can see

55:02.100 --> 55:03.320
flat Cambrian

55:03.320 --> 55:04.440
sedimentary strata

55:04.440 --> 55:05.160
sitting directly

55:05.160 --> 55:06.200
on top of tilted

55:06.200 --> 55:07.060
proterozoic

55:07.060 --> 55:08.320
crystalline basement rock.

55:08.600 --> 55:09.380
There's a physical

55:09.380 --> 55:09.900
gap there

55:09.900 --> 55:10.620
representing over

55:10.620 --> 55:11.300
a billion years

55:11.300 --> 55:12.080
of missing time.

55:12.440 --> 55:13.160
A billion years

55:13.160 --> 55:14.000
where no rock

55:14.000 --> 55:14.620
was preserved.

55:15.180 --> 55:15.880
And while modern

55:15.880 --> 55:16.680
geologists like

55:16.680 --> 55:17.620
Karlstrom debate

55:17.620 --> 55:18.880
the exact synchronicity

55:18.880 --> 55:19.620
of this gap

55:19.620 --> 55:20.320
across the North

55:20.320 --> 55:21.040
American continent,

55:21.560 --> 55:22.200
researchers like

55:22.200 --> 55:23.040
Peters and Gaines

55:23.040 --> 55:23.580
have proposed

55:23.580 --> 55:24.520
something incredible.

55:25.300 --> 55:26.220
They hypothesized

55:26.220 --> 55:26.840
that the massive

55:26.840 --> 55:27.820
weathering and erosion

55:27.820 --> 55:28.320
of rock

55:28.320 --> 55:29.100
during the formation

55:29.100 --> 55:30.500
of the Great Unconformity

55:30.500 --> 55:32.000
actually flushed

55:32.000 --> 55:33.420
so many heavy minerals

55:33.420 --> 55:34.200
like calcium

55:34.200 --> 55:34.800
and phosphorus

55:34.800 --> 55:36.080
into the global oceans

55:36.080 --> 55:37.260
that it fundamentally

55:37.260 --> 55:38.760
changed ocean chemistry.

55:39.480 --> 55:40.320
And that massive

55:40.320 --> 55:41.120
chemical dump

55:41.120 --> 55:41.800
may have helped

55:41.800 --> 55:42.720
trigger the Cambrian

55:42.720 --> 55:43.280
explosion,

55:43.840 --> 55:44.840
the sudden rapid

55:44.840 --> 55:46.140
appearance of complex

55:46.140 --> 55:47.320
hard-shelled life

55:47.320 --> 55:47.860
on Earth.

55:48.020 --> 55:48.300
Wait,

55:48.520 --> 55:49.260
so the gap,

55:49.500 --> 55:50.220
the missing time,

55:50.340 --> 55:51.140
the violent erosion

55:51.140 --> 55:52.800
was actually an active

55:52.800 --> 55:53.920
participant in

55:53.920 --> 55:55.080
planetary continuation.

55:55.920 --> 55:56.820
The breakdown

55:56.820 --> 55:57.860
seeded the minerals

55:57.860 --> 55:59.000
for complex life.

55:59.000 --> 56:00.200
That is the hypothesis.

56:00.520 --> 56:01.360
The boundary wasn't

56:01.360 --> 56:02.500
just a quiet pause

56:02.500 --> 56:03.320
in the rock record.

56:03.780 --> 56:04.660
The violent weathering

56:04.660 --> 56:05.520
process recorded

56:05.520 --> 56:06.160
in that gap

56:06.160 --> 56:07.080
actively changed

56:07.080 --> 56:08.240
the admissibility rules

56:08.240 --> 56:09.380
for biology on Earth.

56:09.760 --> 56:11.020
The breakdown of the rock

56:11.020 --> 56:11.880
allowed the continuation

56:11.880 --> 56:12.580
of life.

56:12.900 --> 56:14.020
That is absolutely astonishing.

56:14.320 --> 56:15.380
Is there any pushback

56:15.380 --> 56:16.000
in geology

56:16.000 --> 56:16.960
against this idea

56:16.960 --> 56:17.860
that gaps are always

56:17.860 --> 56:19.140
so profoundly informative?

56:19.700 --> 56:19.980
Yes,

56:19.980 --> 56:20.860
there is a concept

56:20.860 --> 56:22.020
called paraconformities.

56:22.440 --> 56:23.200
These are gaps

56:23.200 --> 56:24.020
in the rock record

56:24.020 --> 56:24.720
where the layers

56:24.720 --> 56:25.380
above and below

56:25.380 --> 56:26.400
are perfectly parallel

56:26.400 --> 56:27.320
with almost no

56:27.320 --> 56:28.280
erosional relief.

56:28.820 --> 56:29.680
To the naked eye,

56:29.800 --> 56:30.360
it looks like

56:30.360 --> 56:31.500
a continuous sequence.

56:31.660 --> 56:32.160
The only way

56:32.160 --> 56:32.700
you even know

56:32.700 --> 56:33.660
there is missing time

56:33.660 --> 56:34.540
is through microscopic

56:34.540 --> 56:36.460
fossils or radiometric dating.

56:37.140 --> 56:38.220
In these specific cases,

56:38.360 --> 56:39.360
the gap really does

56:39.360 --> 56:40.540
approach pure absence.

56:41.060 --> 56:41.540
It doesn't tell

56:41.540 --> 56:42.420
a dramatic story

56:42.420 --> 56:43.420
of uplift and collision.

56:43.800 --> 56:44.600
It just signifies

56:44.600 --> 56:46.040
a quiet, boring pause

56:46.040 --> 56:46.740
where no BERT

56:46.740 --> 56:47.620
settled for a few

56:47.620 --> 56:48.380
million years.

56:48.940 --> 56:49.800
Affliction acknowledges

56:49.800 --> 56:50.340
this limit.

56:50.600 --> 56:51.320
Not every break

56:51.320 --> 56:52.440
is dramatically articulate.

56:52.940 --> 56:53.600
But the major

56:53.600 --> 56:54.380
unconformities,

56:54.500 --> 56:55.340
the ones that literally

56:55.340 --> 56:56.840
define geological eras,

56:56.940 --> 56:57.580
are profound,

56:57.860 --> 56:58.560
legible records

56:58.560 --> 57:00.020
of inadmissible continuation.

57:00.500 --> 57:00.800
Wow.

57:01.220 --> 57:01.860
Okay, so what does

57:01.860 --> 57:02.700
this all mean for us?

57:02.980 --> 57:03.600
We have covered

57:03.600 --> 57:04.580
an insane amount

57:04.580 --> 57:05.260
of ground today.

57:05.760 --> 57:06.540
But whether you are

57:06.540 --> 57:08.080
a CEO trying to manage

57:08.080 --> 57:09.220
a supply chain crisis,

57:09.660 --> 57:10.820
a lawyer negotiating

57:10.820 --> 57:11.940
a complex contract,

57:12.160 --> 57:12.900
a person just trying

57:12.900 --> 57:13.920
to get a good night's sleep

57:13.920 --> 57:14.780
so your brain

57:14.780 --> 57:15.400
can consolidate

57:15.400 --> 57:16.000
your memories,

57:16.380 --> 57:17.520
a patient relying

57:17.520 --> 57:18.520
on your immune system

57:18.520 --> 57:19.060
to fight off

57:19.060 --> 57:19.920
a novel virus,

57:20.040 --> 57:21.340
or a hiker standing

57:21.340 --> 57:21.740
at the edge

57:21.740 --> 57:22.460
of the Grand Canyon

57:22.460 --> 57:23.540
looking at a billion years

57:23.540 --> 57:24.340
of missing time,

57:24.780 --> 57:25.480
you are witnessing

57:25.480 --> 57:27.620
the exact same universal law

57:27.620 --> 57:28.700
operating in the background.

57:28.820 --> 57:29.940
The law of admissibility

57:29.940 --> 57:30.600
and repair.

57:30.860 --> 57:31.120
Yes.

57:31.980 --> 57:32.720
Complex systems

57:32.720 --> 57:33.960
do not demand perfection.

57:34.500 --> 57:35.360
They do not demand

57:35.360 --> 57:36.440
that nothing ever breaks

57:36.440 --> 57:37.120
or that the data

57:37.120 --> 57:38.560
is always mathematically pure.

57:39.080 --> 57:40.640
They demand a coherent way

57:40.640 --> 57:41.280
to keep going.

57:42.000 --> 57:42.960
They use strict filters

57:42.960 --> 57:44.040
to decide what is allowed

57:44.040 --> 57:44.560
to proceed,

57:44.560 --> 57:46.140
and they rely on messy

57:46.140 --> 57:47.340
active mechanisms

57:47.340 --> 57:48.560
to repair the trajectory

57:48.560 --> 57:50.120
when a massive shock hits.

57:50.460 --> 57:51.040
It is a profound

57:51.040 --> 57:52.060
philosophical shift

57:52.060 --> 57:53.260
from demanding static,

57:53.500 --> 57:54.320
fragile perfection

57:54.320 --> 57:55.560
to managing dynamic,

57:55.840 --> 57:56.560
robust resilience.

57:57.100 --> 57:57.840
And I want to leave you

57:57.840 --> 57:58.740
with a final thought today,

57:59.120 --> 57:59.940
something that wasn't

57:59.940 --> 58:01.720
explicitly in Flictionian's papers,

58:02.200 --> 58:03.260
but builds directly

58:03.260 --> 58:04.540
on this incredible idea.

58:05.460 --> 58:07.260
What about our own

58:07.260 --> 58:08.280
personal identities?

58:09.000 --> 58:10.180
We tend to think of ourselves

58:10.180 --> 58:12.460
as a single static object.

58:13.120 --> 58:14.300
I am who I am,

58:14.560 --> 58:15.880
from childhood to old age.

58:16.220 --> 58:17.180
But what if you

58:17.180 --> 58:19.580
are just an admissibility regime?

58:19.880 --> 58:21.240
That is a very compelling

58:21.240 --> 58:22.580
way to view psychology.

58:22.860 --> 58:23.420
Think about it.

58:23.800 --> 58:25.420
We constantly forget memories

58:25.420 --> 58:26.020
that don't fit

58:26.020 --> 58:26.980
our current self-image.

58:27.460 --> 58:28.720
We repair our worldview

58:28.720 --> 58:29.600
after a trauma,

58:29.820 --> 58:30.380
a heartbreak,

58:30.460 --> 58:31.500
or a massive shock.

58:31.780 --> 58:33.620
We rewrite our personal narratives

58:33.620 --> 58:34.540
to allow ourselves

58:34.540 --> 58:35.600
to step into tomorrow

58:35.600 --> 58:36.880
without collapsing

58:36.880 --> 58:37.500
under the weight

58:37.500 --> 58:38.600
of cognitive dissonance.

58:39.240 --> 58:39.940
You aren't a perfectly

58:39.940 --> 58:40.780
preserved fossil

58:40.780 --> 58:41.580
sitting in a museum.

58:41.940 --> 58:42.920
You are a continuous

58:42.920 --> 58:44.260
living act of repair.

58:44.560 --> 58:45.480
Every single day

58:45.480 --> 58:46.120
you wake up,

58:46.180 --> 58:47.080
your mind and body

58:47.080 --> 58:48.680
run a massive admissibility check,

58:48.800 --> 58:49.620
and you find a way

58:49.620 --> 58:50.120
to continue.

58:50.280 --> 58:51.100
You are a trajectory,

58:51.280 --> 58:51.900
not a snapshot.

58:52.580 --> 58:52.980
Exactly.

58:53.240 --> 58:53.960
So the next time

58:53.960 --> 58:54.760
you face a breakdown

58:54.760 --> 58:55.840
in your own life,

58:56.360 --> 58:56.980
a failure,

58:57.260 --> 58:57.840
a mistake,

58:57.940 --> 58:58.960
a moment of chaos,

58:59.420 --> 59:00.940
maybe don't view it

59:00.940 --> 59:01.500
as a failure

59:01.500 --> 59:02.840
of your entire system.

59:03.300 --> 59:04.460
View it as the raw material

59:04.460 --> 59:05.660
for your next continuation.

59:06.660 --> 59:07.580
The muddy waters

59:07.580 --> 59:08.440
aren't a mistake.

59:08.700 --> 59:09.360
They are exactly

59:09.360 --> 59:10.420
where the repair happens.

59:10.480 --> 59:11.280
Just like that x-ray

59:11.280 --> 59:12.080
at the doctor's office

59:12.080 --> 59:12.760
we started with

59:12.760 --> 59:13.560
a broken bone

59:13.560 --> 59:14.680
doesn't mean the body

59:14.680 --> 59:15.180
has failed.

59:15.400 --> 59:16.320
The break is just

59:16.320 --> 59:16.800
the boundary

59:16.800 --> 59:17.900
where the body begins,

59:18.400 --> 59:19.120
the complex,

59:19.360 --> 59:19.960
messy work

59:19.960 --> 59:20.720
of knitting itself

59:20.720 --> 59:21.300
back together

59:21.300 --> 59:22.000
so it can keep

59:22.000 --> 59:22.740
moving forward.

59:23.000 --> 59:24.320
That captures the spirit

59:24.320 --> 59:25.520
of this research perfectly.

59:25.780 --> 59:26.460
Thank you all

59:26.460 --> 59:27.180
for joining us

59:27.180 --> 59:28.040
on this deep dive.

59:28.220 --> 59:28.840
Keep questioning

59:28.840 --> 59:29.720
the snapshots,

59:29.920 --> 59:30.820
look for the trajectories

59:30.820 --> 59:31.640
in your own life,

59:31.720 --> 59:32.460
and we will see you

59:32.460 --> 59:32.920
next time.

